window.PREP_CONTENT = {"00_Preparation_Plan.md": "# Data Scientist Interview — Preparation Plan\n\nYour interview has two rounds:\n\n| Round | What it tests | Files to use |\n|-------|---------------|--------------|\n| **Round 1 – Concepts** | Statistics, probability, ML, deep learning, SQL, Python, evaluation metrics, A/B testing | `01_DS_Concepts_Notes.md`, `02_DS_Interview_Questions.md` |\n| **Round 2 – Case Study** | Business sense, problem structuring, metrics, model design, experiment design, communication | `03_DS_Case_Study_Questions.md` |\n\n---\n\n## 1. How to use these files\n\n1. **Read `01_DS_Concepts_Notes.md` first** – it is your theory book. Make sure you can explain each concept in 2–3 sentences *in your own words* and give one example.\n2. **Then go through `02_DS_Interview_Questions.md`** – cover the answer, say your answer out loud, then compare. Mark questions you got wrong with ❌ and revisit them.\n3. **Practice `03_DS_Case_Study_Questions.md`** – set a 25–30 minute timer per case, solve it on paper using the framework, *then* read the sample solution.\n4. **Final 2 days** – only revise: the \"Quick Revision Cheat Sheet\" at the end of file 01, your ❌ questions, and 5 case studies.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Suggested 3-week schedule (compress to 1 week if needed)\n\n### Week 1 – Foundations (Round 1)\n| Day | Topic |\n|-----|-------|\n| 1 | Descriptive stats, probability, Bayes theorem, distributions |\n| 2 | Inferential stats: CLT, hypothesis testing, p-values, confidence intervals, t/z/chi-square/ANOVA |\n| 3 | A/B testing end-to-end (sample size, power, pitfalls) |\n| 4 | Linear & logistic regression, assumptions, regularization, bias–variance |\n| 5 | Trees, Random Forest, Boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM), SVM, KNN, Naive Bayes |\n| 6 | Unsupervised: K-Means, hierarchical, DBSCAN, PCA; evaluation metrics |\n| 7 | Revision + 40 questions from file 02 |\n\n### Week 2 – Applied skills\n| Day | Topic |\n|-----|-------|\n| 8 | Feature engineering, missing data, imbalanced data, data leakage, cross-validation |\n| 9 | SQL: joins, GROUP BY, window functions, CTEs — solve 15 problems (LeetCode/StrataScratch) |\n| 10 | Python/Pandas/NumPy — groupby, merge, apply, vectorization |\n| 11 | Deep learning basics: NN, backprop, activation, optimizers, CNN, RNN/LSTM, Transformers |\n| 12 | NLP & GenAI basics (TF-IDF, embeddings, BERT, LLMs, RAG), time series basics |\n| 13 | Model deployment/MLOps basics, recommendation systems |\n| 14 | Mock Round 1 – ask a friend to quiz you from file 02 |\n\n### Week 3 – Case studies (Round 2)\n| Day | Topic |\n|-----|-------|\n| 15 | Learn the case framework; product metric cases (DAU drop, engagement) |\n| 16 | ML system design cases (churn, fraud, recommendation) |\n| 17 | Experimentation cases (A/B test design, launch decisions) |\n| 18 | Business/estimation cases (pricing, guesstimates, marketing ROI) |\n| 19 | Explain your own resume projects as case studies (STAR + metrics) |\n| 20 | 2 full mock case interviews (timed) |\n| 21 | Light revision, rest |\n\n---\n\n## 3. What interviewers actually look for\n\n**Round 1 (Concepts)**\n- Clear, *intuitive* explanations — not memorized definitions.\n- Knowing **when** to use something and its **trade-offs** (e.g., \"Random Forest reduces variance, boosting reduces bias\").\n- Ability to go one level deeper on follow-ups (\"Why does L1 give sparse solutions?\").\n- Connecting concepts to your projects.\n\n**Round 2 (Case Study)**\n- **Asking clarifying questions** before jumping in.\n- **Structured thinking** – say your framework out loud.\n- **Choosing the right metric** and defining it precisely.\n- Practical modeling: data you need, features, baseline, evaluation, deployment, monitoring.\n- **Business impact** – always end with \"what decision does this drive and how do we measure success?\"\n- Mentioning trade-offs, risks, and edge cases.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Golden rules for answering\n\n1. **Structure**: \"I'd approach this in 3 parts: …\"\n2. **Define → Intuition → Example → Trade-off/When to use.**\n3. If you don't know: say what you *do* know, reason from first principles, don't bluff.\n4. Think aloud in case studies — silence is the biggest mistake.\n5. Always **link to your projects** (\"In my churn project, I used SMOTE because …\").\n6. Quantify impact on your resume projects (accuracy ↑, cost ↓, time saved).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Your resume projects — prepare this for each project\n\n| Question | Your answer |\n|----------|-------------|\n| What was the business problem? | |\n| What data did you use? Size? Source? | |\n| How did you clean it / handle missing values? | |\n| What features did you create? | |\n| Which models did you try and why? Baseline? | |\n| Which metric and why? | |\n| Results (numbers)? | |\n| What would you do differently / next? | |\n| Biggest challenge and how you solved it? | |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Practice resources\n- **SQL**: LeetCode Database (Top 50 SQL), StrataScratch, DataLemur\n- **Stats/Prob**: \"Practical Statistics for Data Scientists\" (Bruce), Khan Academy statistics\n- **ML**: \"Hands-On ML\" (Géron), StatQuest videos (Josh Starmer) — excellent for intuition\n- **Case studies**: \"Ace the Data Science Interview\" (Huo & Singh), \"Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments\" (Kohavi) for A/B testing\n- **Mock interviews**: Pramp, peers, record yourself answering\n", "01_DS_Concepts_Notes.md": "# Data Science — Important Concepts (Round 1 Notes)\n\n> How to study: for every concept be able to say **What it is → Intuition → Formula (if any) → When to use → Pros/Cons**.\n\n## Table of Contents\n1. [Statistics — Descriptive](#1-statistics--descriptive)\n2. [Probability](#2-probability)\n3. [Probability Distributions](#3-probability-distributions)\n4. [Inferential Statistics & Hypothesis Testing](#4-inferential-statistics--hypothesis-testing)\n5. [A/B Testing & Experimentation](#5-ab-testing--experimentation)\n6. [Machine Learning Fundamentals](#6-machine-learning-fundamentals)\n7. [Regression Models](#7-regression-models)\n8. [Classification Models](#8-classification-models)\n9. [Tree-Based & Ensemble Models](#9-tree-based--ensemble-models)\n10. [Unsupervised Learning](#10-unsupervised-learning)\n11. [Model Evaluation Metrics](#11-model-evaluation-metrics)\n12. [Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering](#12-data-preprocessing--feature-engineering)\n13. [Model Validation, Tuning & Overfitting](#13-model-validation-tuning--overfitting)\n14. [Deep Learning](#14-deep-learning)\n15. [NLP & Generative AI](#15-nlp--generative-ai)\n16. [Time Series](#16-time-series)\n17. [Recommendation Systems](#17-recommendation-systems)\n18. [SQL for Data Science](#18-sql-for-data-science)\n19. [Python / Pandas / NumPy](#19-python--pandas--numpy)\n20. [MLOps & Deployment Basics](#20-mlops--deployment-basics)\n21. [Linear Algebra & Calculus Essentials](#21-linear-algebra--calculus-essentials)\n22. [Quick Revision Cheat Sheet](#22-quick-revision-cheat-sheet)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Statistics — Descriptive\n\n### Measures of central tendency\n| Measure | Definition | Use when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mean** | Sum / n | Symmetric data, no outliers |\n| **Median** | Middle value of sorted data | Skewed data / outliers (e.g., income) |\n| **Mode** | Most frequent value | Categorical data |\n\n- Right (positive) skew: **Mean > Median > Mode** (long tail on right, e.g., salaries).\n- Left (negative) skew: **Mean < Median < Mode**.\n\n### Measures of spread\n- **Range** = max − min\n- **Variance** σ² = Σ(x − μ)² / n  (sample: divide by **n − 1** → Bessel's correction, makes it unbiased)\n- **Standard deviation** σ = √variance (same unit as data)\n- **IQR** = Q3 − Q1 (robust to outliers)\n- **Coefficient of variation** = σ / μ (compare variability across different scales)\n\n### Outlier detection\n- **IQR rule**: outlier if < Q1 − 1.5·IQR or > Q3 + 1.5·IQR\n- **Z-score**: |z| > 3, where z = (x − μ)/σ\n- Visual: box plot, scatter plot\n- Model based: Isolation Forest, DBSCAN, Local Outlier Factor\n\n### Shape\n- **Skewness**: asymmetry. 0 = symmetric.\n- **Kurtosis**: tailedness. Normal = 3 (excess kurtosis 0). High kurtosis → heavy tails → more extreme values.\n\n### Relationship between variables\n- **Covariance**: direction of linear relationship; unit-dependent; range (−∞, ∞).\n- **Pearson correlation** r = Cov(X,Y)/(σx·σy): strength & direction of *linear* relationship, range [−1, 1].\n- **Spearman correlation**: rank-based; captures *monotonic* relationships; robust to outliers.\n- **Correlation ≠ causation** – confounders, reverse causality, coincidence.\n\n### Percentiles & quantiles\n- Pth percentile: value below which P% of data lies. Median = 50th percentile.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Probability\n\n### Basic rules\n- P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)\n- Independent: P(A ∩ B) = P(A)·P(B)\n- Conditional: P(A|B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)\n- Mutually exclusive: P(A ∩ B) = 0 (mutually exclusive events with non-zero prob are **not** independent)\n- Complement: P(A') = 1 − P(A) → useful for \"at least one\" problems: P(at least one) = 1 − P(none)\n\n### Bayes' Theorem\n```\nP(A|B) = P(B|A) · P(A) / P(B)\nP(B) = P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|A')P(A')     (law of total probability)\n```\n**Classic example** – disease test: prevalence 1%, sensitivity 99%, false positive rate 5%.\nP(disease | +) = 0.99·0.01 / (0.99·0.01 + 0.05·0.99) ≈ **16.7%** → base rate matters!\n\n### Counting\n- Permutations (order matters): nPr = n! / (n−r)!\n- Combinations (order doesn't matter): nCr = n! / (r!(n−r)!)\n\n### Expected value & variance\n- E[X] = Σ x·P(x); E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b; E[X+Y] = E[X] + E[Y] (always)\n- Var(aX + b) = a²Var(X); Var(X+Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y) + 2Cov(X,Y)\n\n### Key theorems\n- **Law of Large Numbers**: sample mean → population mean as n grows.\n- **Central Limit Theorem (CLT)**: distribution of the *sample mean* approaches Normal(μ, σ²/n) as n grows (n ≥ 30 rule of thumb), **regardless of the population distribution** (given finite variance). This is why z/t-tests work.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Probability Distributions\n\n| Distribution | Type | Models | Mean | Variance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bernoulli(p)** | Discrete | Single yes/no trial | p | p(1−p) |\n| **Binomial(n,p)** | Discrete | # successes in n trials | np | np(1−p) |\n| **Poisson(λ)** | Discrete | # events in fixed interval (calls/hr) | λ | λ |\n| **Geometric(p)** | Discrete | # trials until 1st success | 1/p | (1−p)/p² |\n| **Uniform(a,b)** | Continuous | Equally likely values | (a+b)/2 | (b−a)²/12 |\n| **Normal(μ,σ²)** | Continuous | Heights, errors, averages | μ | σ² |\n| **Exponential(λ)** | Continuous | Time between events (memoryless) | 1/λ | 1/λ² |\n| **Log-normal** | Continuous | Income, prices (log is normal) | — | — |\n| **t-distribution** | Continuous | Small-sample means; heavier tails | 0 | >1 |\n| **Chi-square** | Continuous | Sum of squared normals; variance tests, independence | k | 2k |\n\n**Empirical rule (Normal)**: 68% within 1σ, 95% within 2σ (exactly 1.96σ), 99.7% within 3σ.\n\n**Testing normality**: Q-Q plot, histogram, Shapiro-Wilk test, Kolmogorov-Smirnov test.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Inferential Statistics & Hypothesis Testing\n\n### Population vs Sample\n- **Parameter** describes population (μ, σ); **statistic** describes sample (x̄, s).\n- **Standard error** SE = σ / √n — spread of the sampling distribution of the mean.\n\n### Sampling methods\n- Simple random, stratified (divide into groups, sample each), cluster, systematic, convenience (biased).\n- **Biases**: selection bias, survivorship bias, response/non-response bias, sampling bias.\n\n### Hypothesis testing steps\n1. State **H₀** (null: no effect) and **H₁** (alternative).\n2. Choose significance level **α** (usually 0.05).\n3. Choose test & compute test statistic.\n4. Compute **p-value**.\n5. If p < α → reject H₀; else fail to reject H₀ (never \"accept H₀\").\n\n### p-value\n> Probability of observing data **at least as extreme** as what we saw, **assuming H₀ is true**.\n- It is NOT the probability that H₀ is true.\n\n### Errors\n| | H₀ true | H₀ false |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reject H₀ | **Type I error (α)** – false positive | Correct (Power = 1−β) |\n| Fail to reject | Correct | **Type II error (β)** – false negative |\n\n- **Power** = 1 − β = probability of detecting a real effect. Typically target 80%.\n- Power increases with: larger sample size, larger effect size, larger α, lower variance.\n\n### Confidence interval\n- 95% CI = x̄ ± 1.96 · SE\n- Interpretation: if we repeat the sampling many times, 95% of such intervals will contain the true parameter. (Not \"95% probability the parameter lies in this interval\" — that's the Bayesian credible interval.)\n\n### Which test to use?\n| Situation | Test |\n|---|---|\n| Compare mean to known value, σ known / large n | **z-test** |\n| Compare mean, σ unknown / small n | **one-sample t-test** |\n| Compare means of 2 independent groups | **two-sample (independent) t-test** (Welch's if unequal variance) |\n| Same subjects before/after | **paired t-test** |\n| Compare means of 3+ groups | **ANOVA** (then Tukey post-hoc) |\n| Two categorical variables independent? | **Chi-square test of independence** |\n| Observed vs expected frequencies | **Chi-square goodness-of-fit** |\n| Compare proportions (conversion rates) | **two-proportion z-test** |\n| Non-normal data, 2 independent groups | **Mann-Whitney U** |\n| Non-normal paired data | **Wilcoxon signed-rank** |\n| Non-normal 3+ groups | **Kruskal-Wallis** |\n| Compare variances | **F-test / Levene's test** |\n\n### One-tailed vs two-tailed\n- Two-tailed: H₁: μ ≠ μ₀ (any difference). One-tailed: H₁: μ > μ₀ (direction specified in advance).\n\n### Multiple testing problem\n- Running 20 tests at α=0.05 → ~64% chance of at least one false positive (1 − 0.95²⁰).\n- Corrections: **Bonferroni** (α/m, conservative), **Holm**, **Benjamini-Hochberg** (controls False Discovery Rate).\n\n### Parametric vs non-parametric\n- Parametric assumes a distribution (normality) → more power when assumptions hold.\n- Non-parametric: fewer assumptions, rank-based, robust.\n\n### Bayesian vs Frequentist\n- Frequentist: parameters are fixed, data random; uses p-values, CIs.\n- Bayesian: parameters have distributions; prior + likelihood → posterior. Gives \"probability B is better than A\".\n\n---\n\n## 5. A/B Testing & Experimentation\n\n### End-to-end A/B test\n1. **Define the goal & hypothesis** – e.g., \"New checkout button increases conversion\".\n2. **Choose metrics**\n   - **Primary (OEC)**: conversion rate\n   - **Secondary**: revenue per user, AOV\n   - **Guardrail metrics**: latency, crash rate, unsubscribe rate (must not get worse)\n3. **Randomization unit** – usually user (not session/page view) to keep experience consistent.\n4. **Sample size** (per group, proportions):\n   ```\n   n ≈ 16 · σ² / δ²      (rule of thumb for α=0.05, power=0.8)\n   σ² = p(1−p) for proportions, δ = minimum detectable effect (absolute)\n   ```\n   Example: baseline 10%, MDE 1% absolute → n ≈ 16·0.09/0.0001 = 14,400 per group.\n5. **Duration** – at least 1–2 full weeks to capture weekly seasonality; n / daily traffic.\n6. **Run** – no peeking (or use sequential testing).\n7. **Sanity checks** – **Sample Ratio Mismatch (SRM)** check with chi-square; A/A test.\n8. **Analyze** – two-proportion z-test / t-test, CI, practical significance vs statistical significance.\n9. **Decide** – launch, iterate, or kill; consider cost, long-term effects.\n\n### Common pitfalls\n| Pitfall | Explanation | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Peeking** | Stopping as soon as p<0.05 inflates false positives | Fixed horizon or sequential testing (alpha spending) |\n| **Novelty / primacy effect** | Users react to change temporarily | Run longer, analyze by cohort over time |\n| **Network effects / interference** | Treatment affects control (social networks, marketplaces) | Cluster randomization, geo-experiments, switchback tests |\n| **SRM** | Unequal split vs designed → bug in assignment | Chi-square check; don't trust results |\n| **Multiple metrics / variants** | False positives | Bonferroni/BH correction |\n| **Simpson's paradox** | Trend reverses when data combined | Stratify, check segments |\n| **Seasonality / holidays** | Non-representative period | Run full weeks, avoid unusual periods |\n| **Low power** | Can't detect real effect | Bigger sample, variance reduction (**CUPED**) |\n\n### Other experimentation concepts\n- **CUPED**: uses pre-experiment data as covariate to reduce variance → smaller sample needed.\n- **Multi-armed bandits**: dynamically allocate more traffic to the winning variant (explore vs exploit) — good for short-lived decisions (e.g., headlines).\n- **Quasi-experiments** when A/B is impossible: Difference-in-Differences, Regression Discontinuity, Propensity Score Matching, Synthetic Control, Instrumental Variables.\n- **Statistical vs practical significance**: a 0.01% lift might be significant with huge n but not worth engineering cost.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Machine Learning Fundamentals\n\n### Types of learning\n- **Supervised**: labeled data → regression (continuous) / classification (categorical).\n- **Unsupervised**: no labels → clustering, dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection.\n- **Semi-supervised**: few labels + lots of unlabeled.\n- **Self-supervised**: labels generated from data itself (e.g., next-word prediction).\n- **Reinforcement learning**: agent learns via rewards (states, actions, policy).\n\n### Parametric vs non-parametric models\n- Parametric: fixed number of parameters (linear/logistic regression). Fast, strong assumptions.\n- Non-parametric: complexity grows with data (KNN, decision trees). Flexible, need more data.\n\n### Generative vs Discriminative\n- Discriminative models P(y|x) directly — logistic regression, SVM, NN.\n- Generative models P(x|y)P(y) / joint — Naive Bayes, GMM, GANs, LLMs.\n\n### Bias–Variance Trade-off\n```\nExpected error = Bias² + Variance + Irreducible noise\n```\n| | High bias (underfitting) | High variance (overfitting) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Train error | High | Low |\n| Test error | High | High |\n| Fix | More complex model, more features, less regularization | More data, regularization, simpler model, dropout, bagging, early stopping |\n\n### Gradient Descent\n- Update: θ = θ − η · ∇L(θ), η = learning rate.\n- **Batch GD**: whole dataset per step (stable, slow).\n- **Stochastic GD**: one sample (noisy, fast, can escape local minima).\n- **Mini-batch GD**: compromise (standard in DL).\n- Learning rate too high → diverges; too low → slow.\n\n### Loss functions\n| Task | Loss |\n|---|---|\n| Regression | MSE (penalizes large errors), MAE (robust to outliers), Huber (mix) |\n| Binary classification | Binary cross-entropy / log loss |\n| Multi-class | Categorical cross-entropy |\n| SVM | Hinge loss |\n\n### Curse of dimensionality\nAs dimensions increase, data becomes sparse, distances become less meaningful, more data needed → use feature selection, PCA, regularization.\n\n### No Free Lunch theorem\nNo single model is best for all problems → try multiple, validate.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Regression Models\n\n### Linear Regression\n- y = β₀ + β₁x₁ + … + βₙxₙ + ε\n- Fitted by **Ordinary Least Squares** (minimize sum of squared residuals). Closed form: β = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy\n- **Assumptions (LINE + no multicollinearity)**:\n  1. **L**inearity between X and y\n  2. **I**ndependence of errors (no autocorrelation — Durbin-Watson test)\n  3. **N**ormality of residuals (Q-Q plot)\n  4. **E**qual variance — homoscedasticity (residual vs fitted plot)\n  5. No (perfect) **multicollinearity** (check **VIF** > 5–10 is a problem)\n- Coefficient interpretation: one-unit increase in x₁ → β₁ change in y, holding others constant.\n\n### Evaluation\n- **R²** = 1 − SS_res/SS_tot → proportion of variance explained.\n- **Adjusted R²** penalizes adding useless features.\n- MAE, MSE, RMSE, MAPE.\n\n### Regularization\n| | Ridge (L2) | Lasso (L1) | Elastic Net |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Penalty | λΣβ² | λΣ\\|β\\| | mix of both |\n| Effect | Shrinks coefficients toward 0 | Shrinks some exactly to 0 → **feature selection** | Both |\n| Use | Multicollinearity, many small effects | Sparse models, many irrelevant features | Correlated features + selection |\n\n**Why L1 gives sparsity**: the L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes; the loss contours tend to touch it at a corner, where some coefficients are exactly 0.\n\n### Other regression\n- **Polynomial regression** – add x², x³ terms (still linear in parameters).\n- **Quantile regression** – predicts median/percentiles.\n- **Poisson regression** – count data.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Classification Models\n\n### Logistic Regression\n- p = σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(−z)), z = β₀ + βᵀx\n- Models **log-odds**: log(p/(1−p)) = z → linear decision boundary.\n- Loss: log loss (cross-entropy), trained by gradient descent / MLE.\n- Coefficient: e^β = **odds ratio** for one-unit increase.\n- Why not MSE? MSE with sigmoid is non-convex → many local minima.\n- Multi-class: one-vs-rest or **softmax** (multinomial).\n\n### K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)\n- Classify by majority vote of k nearest points (Euclidean, Manhattan, cosine).\n- Lazy learner — no training, slow prediction. **Needs feature scaling.**\n- Small k → overfitting; large k → underfitting. Choose k via CV (odd k for binary).\n\n### Naive Bayes\n- Applies Bayes' theorem assuming features are **conditionally independent** given class.\n- Types: Gaussian (continuous), Multinomial (word counts), Bernoulli (binary).\n- Fast, works well for text/spam. **Laplace smoothing** handles zero probabilities.\n\n### Support Vector Machine (SVM)\n- Finds hyperplane maximizing the **margin** between classes; **support vectors** are points on the margin.\n- **C**: regularization — high C = hard margin (overfit risk), low C = softer margin.\n- **Kernel trick**: implicitly maps data to higher dimension (linear, polynomial, **RBF**). γ in RBF controls reach of each point.\n- Good for high-dimensional data; slow on large datasets; needs scaling.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Tree-Based & Ensemble Models\n\n### Decision Tree\n- Recursively split on feature that best separates the data.\n- Split criteria:\n  - **Gini impurity** = 1 − Σpᵢ²\n  - **Entropy** = −Σpᵢ log₂ pᵢ; **Information Gain** = parent entropy − weighted child entropy\n  - Regression: variance reduction / MSE\n- Pros: interpretable, handles non-linearity, no scaling needed, handles mixed types.\n- Cons: **overfits easily**, unstable (high variance).\n- Control: max_depth, min_samples_split, min_samples_leaf, pruning.\n\n### Ensemble methods\n| | **Bagging** | **Boosting** | **Stacking** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| How | Train models in parallel on bootstrap samples, average/vote | Train sequentially; each model fixes errors of previous | Meta-model learns how to combine base models |\n| Reduces | **Variance** | **Bias** (and variance) | Both |\n| Example | Random Forest | AdaBoost, GBM, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost | — |\n\n### Random Forest\n- Bagging of decision trees + **random subset of features at each split** (decorrelates trees).\n- **OOB (out-of-bag) error**: ~37% of samples not in each bootstrap → free validation.\n- Feature importance: mean decrease in impurity or permutation importance.\n- Robust, little tuning, handles high dimensions.\n\n### Gradient Boosting\n- Each new tree fits the **residuals (negative gradient)** of the current ensemble.\n- Prediction = sum of trees × learning rate.\n- **XGBoost**: regularization (L1/L2), handles missing values, parallel split finding, pruning.\n- **LightGBM**: leaf-wise growth, histogram-based → very fast on big data.\n- **CatBoost**: native categorical handling, ordered boosting.\n- Key hyperparameters: n_estimators, learning_rate, max_depth, subsample, colsample_bytree.\n- AdaBoost: increases weights of misclassified samples.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Unsupervised Learning\n\n### K-Means\n1. Pick k centroids randomly (better: **k-means++**).\n2. Assign each point to nearest centroid.\n3. Recompute centroids as mean.\n4. Repeat until convergence.\n- Minimizes within-cluster sum of squares (inertia).\n- Choose k: **Elbow method**, **Silhouette score** (−1 to 1, higher better), Gap statistic.\n- Limitations: assumes spherical, similar-size clusters; sensitive to outliers & initialization; needs scaling.\n\n### Hierarchical clustering\n- Agglomerative (bottom-up) / divisive. Visualized with **dendrogram**.\n- Linkage: single, complete, average, **Ward**. No need to pre-specify k.\n\n### DBSCAN\n- Density-based: parameters **eps** (radius) and **minPts**.\n- Finds arbitrary-shaped clusters, marks outliers as noise, no need for k.\n- Struggles with varying densities.\n\n### Gaussian Mixture Models\n- Soft clustering (probability of belonging to each cluster), fitted with **EM algorithm**.\n\n### PCA (Principal Component Analysis)\n- Finds orthogonal directions of **maximum variance**.\n- Steps: standardize → covariance matrix → eigenvectors/eigenvalues → sort → project.\n- Explained variance ratio → choose components covering ~90–95% variance.\n- Linear, components lose interpretability; **must scale data first**.\n- Others: **t-SNE**, **UMAP** (non-linear, for visualization), LDA (supervised, maximizes class separation), SVD.\n\n### Anomaly detection\n- Statistical (z-score, IQR), Isolation Forest, One-Class SVM, Autoencoders (high reconstruction error = anomaly).\n\n### Association rules (Market basket)\n- **Support** = P(A∩B), **Confidence** = P(B|A), **Lift** = Confidence / P(B) (>1 = positive association). Apriori algorithm.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Model Evaluation Metrics\n\n### Confusion matrix\n| | Predicted + | Predicted − |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Actual +** | TP | FN (Type II) |\n| **Actual −** | FP (Type I) | TN |\n\n| Metric | Formula | Focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Accuracy** | (TP+TN)/All | Misleading for imbalanced data |\n| **Precision** | TP/(TP+FP) | \"Of predicted positives, how many correct?\" — use when **FP is costly** (spam filter) |\n| **Recall / Sensitivity / TPR** | TP/(TP+FN) | \"Of actual positives, how many found?\" — use when **FN is costly** (cancer, fraud) |\n| **Specificity / TNR** | TN/(TN+FP) | Correct negatives |\n| **F1-score** | 2PR/(P+R) | Harmonic mean, balance P & R |\n| **Fβ** | β>1 weights recall more | |\n| **FPR** | FP/(FP+TN) | x-axis of ROC |\n\n### ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC\n- **ROC curve**: TPR vs FPR across thresholds. **AUC** = probability model ranks a random positive above a random negative. 0.5 = random, 1 = perfect.\n- **PR curve**: precision vs recall — **better for highly imbalanced** data (ROC can look optimistic).\n- **Threshold tuning**: default 0.5 is arbitrary; choose based on business cost.\n\n### Other classification metrics\n- **Log loss** (probability quality), **Brier score**, **Cohen's kappa**, **MCC** (good for imbalance).\n- **Calibration**: are predicted probabilities true probabilities? (reliability plot, Platt scaling, isotonic regression).\n- **Lift / Gain charts** – marketing use.\n- Multi-class: macro avg (equal weight classes), micro avg (equal weight samples), weighted avg.\n\n### Regression metrics\n| Metric | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| MAE | Average absolute error, robust to outliers, interpretable |\n| MSE | Penalizes large errors heavily |\n| RMSE | Same unit as target |\n| MAPE | Percentage error; breaks when actual ≈ 0 |\n| R² / Adjusted R² | Variance explained |\n\n### Ranking metrics\n- Precision@K, Recall@K, **MAP**, **NDCG** (position-aware), MRR, Hit rate.\n\n### Clustering metrics\n- Silhouette score, Davies-Bouldin index, Calinski-Harabasz; with labels: ARI, NMI.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering\n\n### Missing values\n- Types: **MCAR** (completely at random), **MAR** (depends on observed data), **MNAR** (depends on missing value itself).\n- Handling: drop rows/cols (if few), mean/median/mode imputation, KNN imputer, MICE (iterative), model-based, forward fill (time series), add **\"is_missing\" indicator** flag, tree models (XGBoost) handle natively.\n\n### Encoding categorical variables\n| Method | When |\n|---|---|\n| Label / Ordinal encoding | Ordinal categories (low < med < high); fine for trees |\n| One-hot encoding | Nominal, low cardinality (beware dummy variable trap → drop one) |\n| Target / mean encoding | High cardinality; **risk of leakage** → use CV/smoothing |\n| Frequency encoding | High cardinality |\n| Hashing | Very high cardinality |\n| Embeddings | Deep learning |\n\n### Feature scaling\n- **Standardization** (z-score): (x − μ)/σ → mean 0, std 1. For linear models, SVM, PCA, NN, KNN.\n- **Min-Max normalization**: (x − min)/(max − min) → [0,1]. Sensitive to outliers. For NN, images.\n- **Robust scaler**: uses median & IQR → outlier-robust.\n- **Trees don't need scaling.**\n- Fit scaler **only on training data**, then transform test.\n\n### Transformations\n- Log / Box-Cox / Yeo-Johnson for skewed data; binning; polynomial features; interaction features.\n\n### Feature engineering ideas\n- Date: day of week, month, is_weekend, holiday, time since last event.\n- Aggregations: user's avg spend, count of transactions in last 7/30 days (RFM: Recency, Frequency, Monetary).\n- Ratios: price per sqft, clicks/impressions.\n- Text: length, TF-IDF, embeddings.\n- Domain knowledge features are usually the most valuable.\n\n### Feature selection\n- **Filter**: correlation, chi-square, mutual information, variance threshold.\n- **Wrapper**: RFE (recursive feature elimination), forward/backward selection.\n- **Embedded**: Lasso, tree feature importance.\n- Also: SHAP values, permutation importance.\n\n### Imbalanced data\n- Use right metrics (PR-AUC, F1, recall), not accuracy.\n- **Resampling**: random undersampling, oversampling, **SMOTE** (synthetic minority samples via interpolation), ADASYN. Apply **only on training set**.\n- **Class weights** (`class_weight='balanced'`).\n- Threshold tuning, anomaly detection approach, ensemble (BalancedRandomForest).\n\n### Data leakage\n> Information not available at prediction time sneaks into training → overly optimistic results.\n- Examples: scaling before train-test split, target encoding without CV, features created after the event (e.g., \"refund_issued\" in fraud model), random split on time-series data, duplicate rows across train/test.\n- Prevention: split first, use **Pipelines**, time-based splits, check suspiciously high accuracy.\n\n### Multicollinearity\n- Correlated predictors → unstable coefficients, hard interpretation (not a big issue for prediction).\n- Detect: correlation matrix, **VIF**. Fix: drop features, PCA, Ridge.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Model Validation, Tuning & Overfitting\n\n### Splits\n- Train / Validation / Test (e.g., 70/15/15). Test set touched only once at the end.\n- **Stratified split** for imbalanced classification.\n- **Time-based split** for temporal data (train on past, test on future).\n\n### Cross-validation\n- **K-Fold**: split into k folds, train on k−1, validate on 1, repeat k times, average.\n- **Stratified K-Fold**: preserves class ratio.\n- **LOOCV**: k = n (expensive, low bias, high variance).\n- **TimeSeriesSplit / walk-forward**: expanding window.\n- **Group K-Fold**: same user/patient never in both train & val.\n\n### Hyperparameter tuning\n- **Grid search** (exhaustive), **Random search** (often better in high dims), **Bayesian optimization** (Optuna, Hyperopt), early stopping.\n- Parameters are learned (weights); hyperparameters are set before training (learning rate, depth).\n\n### Preventing overfitting\n- More data, data augmentation\n- Regularization (L1/L2), dropout, early stopping\n- Simpler model / pruning / fewer features\n- Cross-validation, ensembling (bagging)\n- Batch normalization (slight regularization effect)\n\n### Interpretability\n- Global: feature importance, partial dependence plots (PDP), coefficients.\n- Local: **SHAP** (Shapley values from game theory – fair contribution of each feature), **LIME** (local linear surrogate).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Deep Learning\n\n### Neural network basics\n- **Neuron**: output = activation(w·x + b).\n- **Forward propagation**: compute predictions layer by layer.\n- **Backpropagation**: compute gradients of loss w.r.t. weights using **chain rule**, then update with gradient descent.\n- **Epoch**: one pass over entire data; **batch size**: samples per update; **iteration**: one update.\n\n### Activation functions\n| Function | Range | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sigmoid | (0,1) | Binary output; vanishing gradient; not zero-centered |\n| Tanh | (−1,1) | Zero-centered; still vanishing gradient |\n| **ReLU** | [0,∞) | Default for hidden layers; fast; \"dying ReLU\" problem |\n| Leaky ReLU / ELU / GELU | | Fixes dying ReLU; GELU used in Transformers |\n| Softmax | (0,1), sums to 1 | Multi-class output |\n\n**Why non-linear activations?** Without them, stacked layers collapse into one linear function.\n\n### Training problems\n- **Vanishing gradients**: gradients shrink in deep nets (sigmoid/tanh) → ReLU, BatchNorm, residual connections, LSTM, good init (He/Xavier).\n- **Exploding gradients**: gradient clipping, proper init.\n- **Overfitting**: dropout, L2 (weight decay), early stopping, data augmentation.\n\n### Optimizers\n- SGD, SGD + **Momentum** (accelerates in consistent direction), **RMSprop** (adaptive LR per parameter), **Adam** (momentum + RMSprop; default choice), AdamW (decoupled weight decay).\n- Learning-rate schedules: step decay, cosine annealing, warmup.\n\n### Normalization\n- **Batch Normalization**: normalizes activations per mini-batch → faster, stable training.\n- **Layer Normalization**: per sample — used in Transformers/RNNs.\n\n### CNN (Convolutional Neural Networks) – images\n- **Convolution layer**: filters/kernels detect local patterns (edges → shapes → objects); parameter sharing.\n- **Stride, padding**; output size = (W − K + 2P)/S + 1.\n- **Pooling** (max/avg): downsampling, translation invariance.\n- Architectures: LeNet, AlexNet, VGG, **ResNet** (skip connections), Inception, EfficientNet.\n- **Transfer learning**: use pretrained model, freeze early layers, fine-tune the top.\n\n### RNN / LSTM / GRU – sequences\n- RNN: hidden state carries info across time steps; suffers vanishing gradients on long sequences.\n- **LSTM**: cell state + gates (forget, input, output) → long-term memory.\n- **GRU**: simpler (update & reset gates), fewer params.\n\n### Transformers\n- **Self-attention**: each token attends to all others: Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ)V.\n- **Multi-head attention**, **positional encoding**, feed-forward layers, residual + LayerNorm.\n- Parallelizable (unlike RNN), captures long-range dependencies.\n- Encoder-only (**BERT** – understanding), decoder-only (**GPT** – generation), encoder-decoder (**T5**, translation).\n\n### Other\n- **Autoencoders**: encoder → bottleneck → decoder; dimensionality reduction, denoising, anomaly detection.\n- **GANs**: generator vs discriminator adversarial training.\n- **Embeddings**: dense vector representations of categorical items/words.\n\n---\n\n## 15. NLP & Generative AI\n\n### Text preprocessing\n- Lowercasing, tokenization, stop-word removal, **stemming** (crude chop: \"running\"→\"run\") vs **lemmatization** (dictionary root: \"better\"→\"good\"), removing punctuation.\n\n### Text representation\n- **Bag of Words**: word counts, ignores order.\n- **TF-IDF**: TF × log(N/df) → down-weights common words.\n- **N-grams**: capture local word order.\n- **Word2Vec** (CBOW / Skip-gram), **GloVe**, FastText (subword) → dense semantic embeddings (\"king − man + woman ≈ queen\").\n- **Contextual embeddings**: BERT — same word gets different vectors in different contexts.\n\n### NLP tasks\n- Sentiment analysis, NER, text classification, topic modeling (**LDA**), summarization, translation, Q&A.\n\n### LLMs & GenAI (increasingly asked)\n- **LLM**: large Transformer pretrained on next-token prediction, then instruction-tuned and aligned (RLHF / DPO).\n- **Tokens, context window, temperature** (randomness), top-k / top-p sampling.\n- **Prompt engineering**: zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought.\n- **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)**: embed documents → store in **vector DB** → retrieve relevant chunks by similarity → pass to LLM as context. Reduces hallucination, uses up-to-date/private data.\n- **Fine-tuning**: full fine-tuning vs parameter-efficient (**LoRA**, QLoRA).\n- **Hallucination**: confident but wrong output → RAG, grounding, evaluation.\n- Evaluation: BLEU, ROUGE (summarization), perplexity, human eval, LLM-as-judge.\n- Cosine similarity = A·B / (|A||B|) — used for embedding search.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Time Series\n\n- **Components**: trend, seasonality, cyclic, noise (irregular).\n- **Decomposition**: additive (y = T + S + R) vs multiplicative (y = T × S × R).\n- **Stationarity**: constant mean, variance, autocorrelation over time. Test: **ADF** (H₀: non-stationary), KPSS. Fix: differencing, log transform.\n- **ACF** (autocorrelation) → helps choose MA order q; **PACF** → AR order p.\n- Models:\n  - Moving average, exponential smoothing, **Holt-Winters** (trend + seasonality)\n  - **ARIMA(p,d,q)**: AR + differencing + MA; **SARIMA** adds seasonality; ARIMAX adds external vars\n  - **Prophet** (trend + seasonality + holidays)\n  - ML: XGBoost with lag features, rolling means; DL: LSTM, Temporal Fusion Transformer\n- **Validation**: never shuffle; walk-forward validation.\n- Metrics: MAE, RMSE, MAPE, sMAPE, MASE.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Recommendation Systems\n\n- **Popularity based**: simple baseline; solves cold start.\n- **Content-based**: recommend items similar to what user liked (item features, TF-IDF, embeddings).\n- **Collaborative filtering**:\n  - User-based (similar users), item-based (similar items) using cosine/Pearson similarity.\n  - **Matrix factorization** (SVD, ALS): user × item matrix ≈ user embeddings × item embeddings.\n- **Hybrid** models; deep learning (two-tower models, neural CF).\n- **Typical architecture**: candidate generation (retrieve ~1000s) → ranking (ML model ranks top 100) → re-ranking (diversity, business rules).\n- **Cold start problem**: new user → popular items, onboarding questions, demographics; new item → content features.\n- Implicit (clicks, views) vs explicit (ratings) feedback.\n- Metrics: Precision@K, Recall@K, NDCG, MAP, coverage, diversity, novelty; online: CTR, conversion, watch time.\n\n---\n\n## 18. SQL for Data Science\n\n### Execution order\n```\nFROM → JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY → LIMIT\n```\n(That's why you can't use a SELECT alias in WHERE.)\n\n### Joins\n- **INNER**: matching rows only; **LEFT**: all from left + matches; **RIGHT**; **FULL OUTER**; **CROSS** (Cartesian); **SELF JOIN**.\n- Anti-join: `LEFT JOIN … WHERE b.id IS NULL` (rows in A not in B).\n\n### WHERE vs HAVING\n- WHERE filters rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.\n\n### Window functions (very frequently asked)\n```sql\nROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC)   -- 1,2,3,4\nRANK()       -- 1,2,2,4 (gaps)\nDENSE_RANK() -- 1,2,2,3 (no gaps)\nLAG(col, 1) OVER (ORDER BY date)  -- previous row value\nLEAD(col, 1)                      -- next row value\nSUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user ORDER BY date) -- running total\nAVG(x) OVER (ORDER BY date ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) -- 7-day moving avg\nNTILE(4) -- quartiles\n```\n\n### Classic queries\n```sql\n-- 2nd highest salary\nSELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp);\n\n-- Nth highest salary per department\nWITH r AS (\n  SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rnk FROM emp)\nSELECT * FROM r WHERE rnk = 2;\n\n-- Duplicates\nSELECT email, COUNT(*) FROM users GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n\n-- Month-over-month growth\nWITH m AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) mon, SUM(amount) rev FROM orders GROUP BY 1)\nSELECT mon, rev, (rev - LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mon)) * 1.0 / LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mon) AS growth\nFROM m;\n\n-- Day-1 retention\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT b.user_id) * 1.0 / COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) AS d1_retention\nFROM signups a LEFT JOIN logins b\n  ON a.user_id = b.user_id AND b.login_date = a.signup_date + INTERVAL '1 day';\n```\n\n### Other\n- `UNION` (removes duplicates) vs `UNION ALL` (keeps, faster).\n- `COUNT(*)` counts all rows; `COUNT(col)` ignores NULLs; `COUNT(DISTINCT col)`.\n- `CASE WHEN` for conditional aggregation: `SUM(CASE WHEN status='done' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)`.\n- `COALESCE(x, 0)` to replace NULL. NULL = NULL is not true → use `IS NULL`.\n- CTE (`WITH`) vs subquery — CTEs are more readable; indexes speed up lookups.\n- `DELETE` (rows, can rollback) vs `TRUNCATE` (all rows, fast) vs `DROP` (table).\n\n---\n\n## 19. Python / Pandas / NumPy\n\n### Python essentials\n- **List** (mutable, ordered), **tuple** (immutable), **set** (unique, unordered), **dict** (key-value, hash map, O(1) lookup).\n- List comprehension: `[x**2 for x in nums if x > 0]`\n- `lambda`, `map`, `filter`, `zip`, `enumerate`.\n- `*args` (variable positional), `**kwargs` (variable keyword).\n- **Shallow vs deep copy** (`copy.copy` vs `copy.deepcopy`).\n- **Generators** (`yield`) — lazy, memory efficient.\n- **Decorators** — functions that wrap other functions.\n- `is` (identity) vs `==` (equality).\n- GIL — limits multi-threading for CPU-bound tasks → use multiprocessing.\n\n### NumPy\n- Vectorized operations are much faster than loops; **broadcasting** rules.\n- `np.array`, `reshape`, `np.dot` / `@`, `axis=0` (down rows / per column), `axis=1` (across columns / per row).\n\n### Pandas must-know\n```python\ndf.head(), df.info(), df.describe(), df.shape, df.dtypes\ndf.isnull().sum()\ndf.fillna(df['col'].median()); df.dropna()\ndf.drop_duplicates()\ndf.loc[row_label, col_label]   # label based\ndf.iloc[0:5, 1:3]              # position based\ndf[df['age'] > 30]\ndf.groupby('city')['sales'].agg(['mean', 'sum', 'count'])\ndf.merge(df2, on='id', how='left')      # SQL-like join\npd.concat([df1, df2], axis=0)\ndf.pivot_table(values='sales', index='region', columns='month', aggfunc='sum')\ndf['col'].value_counts(normalize=True)\ndf['new'] = df['col'].apply(lambda x: x * 2)\ndf.sort_values('col', ascending=False)\ndf['rank'] = df.groupby('dept')['salary'].rank(ascending=False)\ndf['rolling'] = df['sales'].rolling(7).mean()\npd.to_datetime(df['date']); df['date'].dt.month\n```\n- `apply` is slow → prefer vectorized operations.\n- `merge` (on columns) vs `join` (on index) vs `concat` (stack).\n\n### Scikit-learn workflow\n```python\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score, GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\n\npre = ColumnTransformer([\n    ('num', StandardScaler(), num_cols),\n    ('cat', OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown='ignore'), cat_cols)])\npipe = Pipeline([('pre', pre), ('model', LogisticRegression())])\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=42)\npipe.fit(X_tr, y_tr)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 20. MLOps & Deployment Basics\n\n- **Model lifecycle**: problem → data → features → train → evaluate → deploy → monitor → retrain.\n- **Deployment modes**: batch (nightly scores), real-time API (Flask/FastAPI + Docker), streaming, edge.\n- **Tools**: Docker, Kubernetes, MLflow (tracking/registry), Airflow (pipelines), DVC (data versioning), cloud (AWS SageMaker, GCP Vertex AI, Azure ML).\n- **Monitoring**:\n  - **Data drift**: input distribution changes (PSI, KS test).\n  - **Concept drift**: relationship between X and y changes (e.g., fraud patterns change).\n  - Model performance, latency, errors.\n- **Feature store**: consistent features for training & serving (avoid **training-serving skew**).\n- **Shadow deployment, canary release, A/B test** new model vs old.\n- Model versioning, reproducibility (random seeds, environment).\n\n---\n\n## 21. Linear Algebra & Calculus Essentials\n\n- **Vectors, matrices, dot product** (similarity / projection), matrix multiplication (m×n · n×p = m×p).\n- **Transpose, inverse** (only square, non-singular), **determinant** (0 → singular).\n- **Rank**: number of linearly independent rows/columns.\n- **Eigenvectors/eigenvalues**: Av = λv — direction unchanged by transformation; used in PCA.\n- **SVD**: A = UΣVᵀ — used in PCA, recommender systems, compression.\n- **Norms**: L1 = Σ|x|, L2 = √Σx².\n- **Derivatives, partial derivatives, gradient** (vector of partials, points to steepest ascent), **chain rule** (basis of backprop).\n- **Convex function**: any local minimum is global (linear/logistic regression loss is convex; NN loss is not).\n- **MLE (Maximum Likelihood Estimation)**: choose parameters that maximize probability of observed data. MSE = MLE under Gaussian noise; log-loss = MLE under Bernoulli.\n- **MAP**: MLE + prior → L2 regularization = Gaussian prior, L1 = Laplace prior.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Quick Revision Cheat Sheet\n\n| Question | One-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| p-value | P(data this extreme \\| H₀ true) |\n| Type I / II | False positive / false negative |\n| Power | P(reject H₀ \\| H₁ true), 1−β, aim 0.8 |\n| CLT | Sample means ~ Normal for large n regardless of population |\n| Bias–variance | Simple = high bias; complex = high variance |\n| L1 vs L2 | L1 → sparse (feature selection); L2 → shrink, handles multicollinearity |\n| Bagging vs boosting | Parallel, ↓variance vs sequential, ↓bias |\n| RF vs GBM | RF robust, less tuning; GBM usually more accurate, needs tuning |\n| Precision vs recall | FP costly → precision; FN costly → recall |\n| ROC vs PR AUC | Imbalanced → PR-AUC |\n| Imbalanced data | Class weights, SMOTE (train only), threshold, right metric |\n| Data leakage | Future/target info in training; split first, pipelines |\n| Missing data | Drop, impute (median/KNN/MICE), indicator, tree-native |\n| Scaling needed? | Yes: KNN, SVM, PCA, NN, regularized linear. No: trees |\n| K-Means k | Elbow, silhouette |\n| PCA | Orthogonal max-variance directions; scale first |\n| Vanishing gradient | ReLU, BatchNorm, ResNet skip, LSTM |\n| Dropout | Randomly zero neurons during training → regularization |\n| Attention | softmax(QKᵀ/√d)V |\n| RAG | Retrieve relevant docs → feed to LLM |\n| A/B sample size | n ≈ 16σ²/δ² per group |\n| Peeking | Inflates false positives |\n| SRM | Unequal traffic split → assignment bug |\n| Simpson's paradox | Aggregated trend reverses in subgroups |\n| Stationarity | Constant mean/var; ADF test; differencing |\n| RANK vs DENSE_RANK | 1,2,2,4 vs 1,2,2,3 |\n| WHERE vs HAVING | Before vs after GROUP BY |\n", "02_DS_Interview_Questions.md": "# Data Scientist Interview Questions with Answers (Round 1 — Concepts)\n\n> **How to practice**: read the question, **answer out loud first**, then check the answer. Mark weak ones with ❌ and revisit.\n> Difficulty: 🟢 Easy · 🟡 Medium · 🔴 Hard\n\n## Sections\n1. [Statistics](#1-statistics)\n2. [Probability (with puzzles)](#2-probability)\n3. [Hypothesis Testing & A/B Testing](#3-hypothesis-testing--ab-testing)\n4. [ML Fundamentals](#4-ml-fundamentals)\n5. [Regression](#5-regression)\n6. [Classification & Algorithms](#6-classification--algorithms)\n7. [Trees & Ensembles](#7-trees--ensembles)\n8. [Unsupervised Learning](#8-unsupervised-learning)\n9. [Evaluation Metrics](#9-evaluation-metrics)\n10. [Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering](#10-data-preprocessing--feature-engineering)\n11. [Deep Learning](#11-deep-learning)\n12. [NLP & GenAI](#12-nlp--genai)\n13. [Time Series](#13-time-series)\n14. [SQL (with queries)](#14-sql)\n15. [Python & Pandas](#15-python--pandas)\n16. [MLOps / Deployment](#16-mlops--deployment)\n17. [Project & HR-style Technical Questions](#17-project--behavioral-technical-questions)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Statistics\n\n**Q1. 🟢 What is the difference between mean, median and mode? When would you use median over mean?**\nMean = average, median = middle value, mode = most frequent. Use median when data is skewed or has outliers (e.g., house prices, salaries) because the mean gets pulled toward extreme values.\n\n**Q2. 🟢 What is standard deviation vs variance?**\nVariance is the average squared deviation from the mean; SD is its square root, so it's in the same unit as the data and easier to interpret.\n\n**Q3. 🟡 Why do we divide by n−1 for sample variance?**\nBecause we use the sample mean (estimated from the same data), which makes deviations slightly smaller than they'd be from the true mean. Dividing by n−1 (Bessel's correction) makes the estimator unbiased. We lose one degree of freedom.\n\n**Q4. 🟢 What is skewness? Give a real example of right-skewed data.**\nAsymmetry of a distribution. Right-skewed: long tail on the right — income, house prices, time on website. Mean > median.\n\n**Q5. 🟡 How do you detect and treat outliers?**\nDetect: box plot/IQR rule, z-score > 3, scatter plots, Isolation Forest/DBSCAN. Treat: first check if it's an error (fix/remove) or genuine (keep). Options: cap/winsorize, log transform, use robust models/metrics (median, MAE, tree models), or model separately. Never blindly delete — in fraud detection outliers are the signal.\n\n**Q6. 🟢 Difference between covariance and correlation?**\nCovariance shows direction of linear relationship but depends on units. Correlation is standardized covariance (−1 to 1), unit-free, shows both strength and direction.\n\n**Q7. 🟡 Pearson vs Spearman correlation?**\nPearson measures linear relationship, sensitive to outliers. Spearman uses ranks → measures monotonic relationship, robust to outliers and works for ordinal data.\n\n**Q8. 🟢 Correlation vs causation — give an example.**\nIce cream sales and drowning deaths are correlated, but both are caused by hot weather (confounder). To establish causation: randomized experiments, or causal inference methods (DiD, IV, matching).\n\n**Q9. 🟡 What is the Central Limit Theorem and why is it important?**\nThe distribution of sample means approaches a normal distribution as sample size increases (n≥30 typically), regardless of the population's distribution, with mean μ and SE σ/√n. It allows us to use z/t-tests and build confidence intervals even when the data isn't normal — the foundation of A/B testing.\n\n**Q10. 🟡 What is the Law of Large Numbers? How is it different from CLT?**\nLLN: the sample mean converges to the population mean as n grows. CLT: describes the *shape* (normal) of the distribution of the sample mean. LLN is about the value; CLT is about the distribution.\n\n**Q11. 🟢 What is a normal distribution? Properties?**\nBell-shaped, symmetric, mean = median = mode, defined by μ and σ. 68-95-99.7 rule.\n\n**Q12. 🟡 How would you check if data is normally distributed?**\nHistogram, Q-Q plot (points on straight line), skewness/kurtosis values, Shapiro-Wilk test (small n), Kolmogorov-Smirnov / Anderson-Darling.\n\n**Q13. 🟡 What are different types of sampling? What is sampling bias?**\nSimple random, stratified, cluster, systematic, convenience. Sampling bias: sample doesn't represent population (e.g., online survey excludes people without internet).\n\n**Q14. 🟡 What is selection bias and survivorship bias?**\nSelection bias: non-random selection makes sample unrepresentative. Survivorship bias: only looking at \"survivors\" — e.g., WWII planes: armor was added where returning planes were *not* hit, because planes hit there didn't return.\n\n**Q15. 🟢 What is a confidence interval? Interpret a 95% CI.**\nA range likely to contain the true parameter. If we repeated sampling 100 times, about 95 of the computed intervals would contain the true value.\n\n**Q16. 🟡 What is Simpson's paradox?**\nA trend that appears in several groups reverses when the groups are combined, due to a confounding variable with unequal group sizes. Example: Treatment A has better success rate for both small and large kidney stones, but B looks better overall because B was given mostly to easy (small stone) cases.\n\n**Q17. 🔴 What is the difference between a standard deviation and standard error?**\nSD measures spread of individual data points. SE measures spread of a sample statistic (e.g., mean) across samples: SE = SD/√n. SE decreases with larger n; SD doesn't.\n\n**Q18. 🟡 What is a long-tailed distribution? Why does it matter?**\nMany rare events with a few extremely frequent ones (e.g., product sales, word frequencies). Matters for: choosing metrics (median), modeling (log transform), recommendation (long-tail items), and classification (rare classes).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Probability\n\n**Q19. 🟢 Explain Bayes' theorem with an example.**\nP(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). Disease test: 1% prevalence, 99% sensitivity, 5% FPR. P(disease|+) = (0.99×0.01)/(0.99×0.01 + 0.05×0.99) ≈ 16.7%. Even with an accurate test, a positive result is mostly false because the disease is rare.\n\n**Q20. 🟢 Two dice are rolled. Probability the sum is 7?**\nFavorable: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1) = 6 out of 36 = **1/6**.\n\n**Q21. 🟢 Probability of at least one head in 3 coin flips?**\n1 − P(no heads) = 1 − (1/2)³ = **7/8**.\n\n**Q22. 🟡 A family has two children. At least one is a boy. Probability both are boys?**\nSample space: BB, BG, GB (GG excluded). **1/3**.\n\n**Q23. 🟡 Monty Hall problem — should you switch?**\nYes. Initially you pick the car with 1/3. The host, who knows, opens a goat door. Switching wins with probability **2/3**.\n\n**Q24. 🟡 You flip a fair coin 10 times. Probability of exactly 5 heads?**\nC(10,5)/2¹⁰ = 252/1024 ≈ **24.6%**.\n\n**Q25. 🟡 Expected number of rolls of a die to get a 6?**\nGeometric distribution with p=1/6 → E = 1/p = **6**.\n\n**Q26. 🟡 Birthday problem: How many people for >50% chance two share a birthday?**\n**23** people. P(no match) = 365/365 × 364/365 × … ; drops below 0.5 at n=23.\n\n**Q27. 🔴 A bag has 3 red and 2 blue balls. Draw 2 without replacement. P(both red)?**\n(3/5)×(2/4) = **3/10**.\n\n**Q28. 🔴 You have a coin; you suspect it's biased. 10 flips gives 8 heads. Is it biased?**\nH₀: p=0.5. P(≥8 heads) = (C(10,8)+C(10,9)+C(10,10))/1024 = (45+10+1)/1024 ≈ 0.055 (one-tailed); two-tailed ≈ 0.11. Not significant at α=0.05 → can't conclude bias; need more flips.\n\n**Q29. 🟡 What is the difference between independent and mutually exclusive events?**\nIndependent: one doesn't affect the other's probability, P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B). Mutually exclusive: can't happen together, P(A∩B)=0. If both have non-zero probability, mutually exclusive events are dependent (if A happens, B certainly doesn't).\n\n**Q30. 🟡 When would you use Poisson vs Binomial distribution?**\nBinomial: fixed number of trials n, count successes (e.g., 10 emails, how many opened). Poisson: count of events in a fixed interval with no fixed n (e.g., customers per hour). Poisson approximates Binomial when n is large and p small.\n\n**Q31. 🔴 A stick is broken at two random points. Probability the three pieces form a triangle?**\n**1/4**. (Each piece must be less than half the length.)\n\n**Q32. 🔴 Expected number of coin flips to get two heads in a row?**\n**6**. Let E = expected flips. Set up states: E = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E, E₁ = 1 + ½·0 + ½E → solving gives E = 6.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hypothesis Testing & A/B Testing\n\n**Q33. 🟢 What is a p-value? Explain to a non-technical person.**\nTechnical: probability of seeing results at least this extreme if the null hypothesis were true. Simple: \"If the new feature truly had no effect, how surprising would our results be? A small p-value means very surprising, so we believe the feature probably has an effect.\"\n\n**Q34. 🟢 What are Type I and Type II errors? Which is worse?**\nType I: false positive (convict an innocent person). Type II: false negative (let a guilty person go). Which is worse depends on context — in cancer screening Type II is worse; in spam filtering (blocking important email) Type I is worse.\n\n**Q35. 🟡 What is statistical power? How do you increase it?**\nProbability of detecting a true effect (1−β). Increase by: larger sample, larger effect size, higher α, lower variance (CUPED, better metrics), one-tailed test.\n\n**Q36. 🟡 When do you use a t-test vs z-test?**\nz-test: population SD known or large n (>30). t-test: SD unknown and small sample; t-distribution has heavier tails. For large n they converge.\n\n**Q37. 🟡 What test would you use to compare conversion rates of two groups?**\nTwo-proportion z-test (or chi-square test on a 2×2 table — equivalent).\n\n**Q38. 🟡 What is ANOVA? Why not use multiple t-tests?**\nANOVA tests whether means of 3+ groups differ, using ratio of between-group to within-group variance (F-statistic). Multiple t-tests inflate the Type I error rate. After significant ANOVA, use post-hoc tests (Tukey HSD).\n\n**Q39. 🟡 What is a chi-square test used for?**\nTest of independence between two categorical variables (e.g., gender vs product preference), or goodness-of-fit (observed vs expected frequencies). Also used for SRM check in A/B testing.\n\n**Q40. 🟡 Walk me through how you would design an A/B test.**\n1) Define hypothesis & business goal. 2) Choose primary metric + guardrail metrics. 3) Choose randomization unit (user). 4) Calculate sample size from baseline, MDE, α=0.05, power=0.8. 5) Decide duration (≥1–2 full weeks). 6) Randomly split, run without peeking. 7) Sanity checks (SRM, A/A, invariant metrics). 8) Analyze with appropriate test, CI, segment analysis. 9) Make decision considering practical significance and costs.\n\n**Q41. 🔴 How do you calculate sample size for an A/B test?**\nInputs: baseline rate, minimum detectable effect, α, power. Rule of thumb n ≈ 16·p(1−p)/δ² per group. E.g., baseline 5%, detect 0.5% absolute lift: 16×0.0475/0.000025 ≈ 30,400 per group.\n\n**Q42. 🔴 What is the problem with \"peeking\" at A/B test results?**\nChecking repeatedly and stopping when p<0.05 dramatically increases false positives (could be 20–30% instead of 5%), because random fluctuations will cross the threshold at some point. Fix: pre-define sample size, or use sequential testing (O'Brien-Fleming, alpha spending, always-valid p-values).\n\n**Q43. 🔴 Your A/B test shows a significant lift, but the PM is skeptical. What would you check?**\nSRM (traffic split correct?), A/A test results, novelty effect (lift decaying over time?), test duration covering weekly cycle, multiple testing, segment consistency, outliers driving the mean, instrumentation bugs, guardrail metrics, whether the effect is practically significant.\n\n**Q44. 🔴 What are network effects in A/B testing and how do you handle them?**\nWhen treatment users influence control users (e.g., a new messaging feature in a social app; ride-sharing marketplace where treatment drivers take riders from control). Violates independence (SUTVA). Solutions: cluster-based randomization (by friend graph cluster), geo/market-level randomization, switchback experiments (alternate treatment over time).\n\n**Q45. 🟡 What is a novelty effect?**\nUsers initially engage more with a new feature just because it's new; effect fades. Detect by plotting the treatment effect over time or analyzing new vs returning users. Run tests longer.\n\n**Q46. 🔴 What if you can't run an A/B test? How do you measure impact?**\nQuasi-experimental methods: Difference-in-Differences (compare change in treated vs untreated region before/after), Regression Discontinuity, Propensity Score Matching, Synthetic Control, Interrupted Time Series, Instrumental Variables.\n\n**Q47. 🟡 What is the multiple testing problem and how to fix it?**\nMore tests → higher chance of false positives. With 20 tests at α=0.05, P(≥1 false positive) ≈ 64%. Fix: Bonferroni (α/m), Holm, Benjamini-Hochberg (FDR control).\n\n**Q48. 🟡 Statistical significance vs practical significance?**\nStatistically significant = unlikely due to chance. Practically significant = large enough to matter for the business. With millions of users, a 0.01% lift can be significant but not worth the engineering/maintenance cost.\n\n**Q49. 🔴 What is CUPED?**\nControlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data. Adjusts the metric using a pre-experiment covariate (e.g., user's past spend): Y_adj = Y − θ(X − mean(X)). Reduces variance → smaller sample sizes / faster tests.\n\n**Q50. 🟡 What are guardrail metrics?**\nMetrics that must not degrade even if the primary metric improves — e.g., page load time, crash rate, unsubscribe rate, revenue. They protect against harmful launches.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ML Fundamentals\n\n**Q51. 🟢 What is the difference between supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning?**\nSupervised: learn mapping from labeled inputs to outputs (spam detection). Unsupervised: find structure in unlabeled data (customer segmentation). RL: agent learns actions through rewards (game playing, robotics).\n\n**Q52. 🟢 Regression vs classification?**\nRegression predicts continuous values (price); classification predicts categories (spam/not spam).\n\n**Q53. 🟡 Explain the bias-variance trade-off.**\nBias: error from overly simple assumptions (underfitting). Variance: error from sensitivity to training data fluctuations (overfitting). As complexity increases, bias falls and variance rises. Goal: sweet spot minimizing total error. Example: linear model on curved data = high bias; deep unpruned tree = high variance.\n\n**Q54. 🟢 What is overfitting and how do you prevent it?**\nModel learns noise in training data → great train performance, poor test performance. Prevent: more data, cross-validation, regularization (L1/L2), simpler model, pruning, dropout, early stopping, feature selection, ensembling (bagging), data augmentation.\n\n**Q55. 🟡 How do you know if your model is overfitting or underfitting?**\nCompare training vs validation error / learning curves. Overfitting: low train error, high val error (big gap). Underfitting: both high.\n\n**Q56. 🟡 What is regularization? Why does it work?**\nAdding a penalty for model complexity (large weights) to the loss function. It constrains the model, preventing it from fitting noise, trading a bit of bias for a large reduction in variance.\n\n**Q57. 🟡 Explain gradient descent. What are its variants?**\nIterative optimization: move parameters in the direction of negative gradient of the loss, step size = learning rate. Variants: batch (full data), stochastic (1 sample), mini-batch. Advanced: momentum, RMSprop, Adam.\n\n**Q58. 🟡 What happens if the learning rate is too high or too low?**\nToo high: overshoots minimum, oscillates or diverges. Too low: very slow convergence, can get stuck in plateaus. Use LR schedules or adaptive optimizers.\n\n**Q59. 🟡 What is the curse of dimensionality?**\nAs the number of features grows, data becomes sparse, distances between points become similar (distance-based methods like KNN fail), and you need exponentially more data. Fix: feature selection, PCA, regularization.\n\n**Q60. 🟡 Parametric vs non-parametric models?**\nParametric: fixed number of parameters, assumes functional form (linear regression). Non-parametric: no fixed form, complexity grows with data (KNN, decision trees, kernel SVM).\n\n**Q61. 🔴 Generative vs discriminative models?**\nDiscriminative learn P(y|x) — decision boundary (logistic regression, SVM). Generative learn P(x,y) or P(x|y) — how data is generated (Naive Bayes, GMM, HMM, GANs). Generative can generate new samples and work with less data; discriminative are usually more accurate for classification.\n\n**Q62. 🟡 What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?**\nParameters are learned from data (weights, coefficients). Hyperparameters are set before training (learning rate, k in KNN, max_depth) and tuned via validation.\n\n**Q63. 🔴 What is MLE (Maximum Likelihood Estimation)?**\nChoose parameters that maximize the likelihood of the observed data. Linear regression with Gaussian noise → MLE equals minimizing MSE. Logistic regression → MLE equals minimizing log loss.\n\n**Q64. 🟡 How do you choose which algorithm to use?**\nDepends on: problem type, data size, number of features, interpretability needs, latency, linearity, data types. Start with a simple baseline (logistic/linear regression), then try tree ensembles (RF, XGBoost) for tabular data, deep learning for images/text/audio. Validate with CV and pick based on business metric + constraints.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Regression\n\n**Q65. 🟢 What are the assumptions of linear regression?**\nLinearity, independence of errors, homoscedasticity (constant error variance), normality of residuals, no multicollinearity.\n\n**Q66. 🟡 What happens if these assumptions are violated?**\nNon-linearity → biased predictions (add polynomial terms/transform). Heteroscedasticity → unreliable standard errors/p-values (use log transform, weighted least squares, robust SEs). Autocorrelation → underestimated SEs (time-series models). Multicollinearity → unstable coefficients (drop features, Ridge, PCA). Non-normal residuals → affects inference in small samples.\n\n**Q67. 🟡 What is R²? Can it be negative?**\nProportion of variance in y explained by the model. On training data with intercept, it's between 0 and 1. On test data it can be negative — model is worse than predicting the mean.\n\n**Q68. 🟡 R² vs Adjusted R²?**\nR² never decreases when adding features (even useless ones). Adjusted R² penalizes the number of predictors and only increases if the new feature improves the model more than expected by chance.\n\n**Q69. 🟡 What is multicollinearity? How do you detect and handle it?**\nHigh correlation between independent variables → unstable, high-variance coefficients, hard to interpret. Detect: correlation matrix, VIF > 5–10. Handle: remove one of the correlated features, combine them, PCA, Ridge regression.\n\n**Q70. 🟡 L1 vs L2 regularization? Why does L1 produce sparse solutions?**\nL1 (Lasso) adds λΣ|β|; L2 (Ridge) adds λΣβ². L1 can shrink coefficients exactly to zero → feature selection. Geometrically, the L1 constraint is a diamond with corners on axes; the elliptical loss contours usually touch the diamond at a corner where some coefficients = 0. L2's circle has no corners, so coefficients become small but not zero.\n\n**Q71. 🟡 How do you interpret coefficients in linear regression? And with log-transformed target?**\nβ₁: one unit increase in x₁ changes y by β₁, others constant. If y is log-transformed: one-unit increase in x changes y by about (e^β − 1)×100%.\n\n**Q72. 🟡 MAE vs MSE vs RMSE — which to use?**\nMAE: robust to outliers, all errors weighted equally. MSE/RMSE: penalize large errors more — use when large errors are particularly bad. RMSE is in the target's units.\n\n**Q73. 🔴 Why can't we use linear regression for classification?**\nOutputs aren't bounded to [0,1], sensitive to outliers shifting the decision boundary, errors aren't normally distributed, and MSE is not appropriate for probabilities. Use logistic regression.\n\n**Q74. 🟡 What are residuals? What does a residual plot tell you?**\nResidual = actual − predicted. A good residual plot shows random scatter around 0. Patterns (curve) → non-linearity; funnel shape → heteroscedasticity.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Classification & Algorithms\n\n**Q75. 🟢 How does logistic regression work?**\nComputes a linear combination of features, passes it through a sigmoid to get a probability between 0 and 1, and classifies with a threshold. It models the log-odds as linear in features. Trained by maximizing likelihood (minimizing log loss).\n\n**Q76. 🟡 Why is logistic regression called \"regression\"?**\nIt regresses the log-odds of the outcome on the features (a linear regression on the logit scale); classification only happens after applying a threshold.\n\n**Q77. 🟡 How to interpret logistic regression coefficients?**\nβ = change in log-odds per unit increase of x. e^β = odds ratio; e.g., β=0.7 → e^0.7≈2 → odds double.\n\n**Q78. 🟡 Why not use MSE as loss for logistic regression?**\nWith sigmoid, MSE makes the loss non-convex (multiple local minima) and gives weak gradients. Log loss is convex and penalizes confident wrong predictions heavily.\n\n**Q79. 🟡 How does KNN work? How do you choose k?**\nFor a new point, find k closest training points (by distance) and take majority vote (classification) or average (regression). Choose k via cross-validation; small k = overfitting/noise-sensitive, large k = oversmoothing. Must scale features.\n\n**Q80. 🟡 What is \"naive\" in Naive Bayes? When does it work well?**\nIt assumes features are conditionally independent given the class (rarely true). Still works well for text classification/spam, high-dimensional data, small datasets, and is very fast.\n\n**Q81. 🟡 Explain SVM. What is the kernel trick?**\nSVM finds the hyperplane maximizing the margin between classes; only support vectors (points closest to boundary) define it. Kernel trick computes dot products in a higher-dimensional space without explicitly transforming data, allowing non-linear boundaries (RBF, polynomial).\n\n**Q82. 🟡 What does the C parameter in SVM do?**\nControls trade-off between maximizing margin and minimizing misclassification. High C = penalize errors heavily → narrow margin, risk of overfitting. Low C = wider margin, more tolerance → may underfit.\n\n**Q83. 🔴 Logistic regression vs SVM vs Decision Tree — when to use which?**\nLogistic: need probabilities, interpretability, linearly separable, baseline. SVM: high-dimensional, clear margin, small/medium datasets. Trees: non-linear relationships, mixed data types, interpretability via rules; ensembles of trees for best tabular performance.\n\n**Q84. 🔴 How would you handle multi-class classification?**\nNatively multi-class algorithms (trees, Naive Bayes, softmax regression, NN) or decompose: One-vs-Rest (k classifiers) or One-vs-One (k(k−1)/2 classifiers). Evaluate with macro/micro/weighted F1 and a confusion matrix.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Trees & Ensembles\n\n**Q85. 🟢 How does a decision tree decide where to split?**\nTries all features and thresholds, picks the split that maximizes impurity reduction — Gini impurity or entropy/information gain for classification, variance reduction (MSE) for regression.\n\n**Q86. 🟡 Gini vs Entropy?**\nBoth measure impurity. Gini = 1−Σp², entropy = −Σp log p. Gini is slightly faster (no log); results are usually very similar.\n\n**Q87. 🟡 Why do decision trees overfit? How do you prevent it?**\nThey can keep splitting until every leaf is pure, memorizing noise. Prevent: max_depth, min_samples_leaf, min_samples_split, max_leaf_nodes, pruning (cost complexity), or use ensembles.\n\n**Q88. 🟡 Bagging vs Boosting?**\nBagging: train many models independently on bootstrap samples in parallel, average them → reduces variance (Random Forest). Boosting: train models sequentially, each focusing on previous errors → reduces bias (XGBoost). Boosting is more prone to overfitting noise and needs careful tuning.\n\n**Q89. 🟡 How does Random Forest work? Why is it better than a single tree?**\nBuilds many decision trees on bootstrap samples and at each split considers a random subset of features; aggregates by vote/average. Randomness decorrelates trees, so averaging reduces variance significantly while keeping bias low.\n\n**Q90. 🟡 What is out-of-bag (OOB) error?**\nEach tree's bootstrap sample excludes ~37% of rows. Predicting those rows with trees that didn't see them gives a free validation estimate.\n\n**Q91. 🔴 How does Gradient Boosting work?**\nStart with a simple prediction (mean). Compute residuals (negative gradients of loss). Fit a small tree to the residuals. Add it to the model multiplied by learning rate. Repeat. Final prediction = sum of all trees.\n\n**Q92. 🔴 What makes XGBoost better than standard GBM?**\nRegularization (L1/L2 on leaf weights), second-order gradient (Newton) optimization, built-in missing value handling, parallelized split finding, tree pruning with max_depth & gamma, column/row subsampling, efficient cache usage.\n\n**Q93. 🟡 XGBoost vs LightGBM vs CatBoost?**\nXGBoost: level-wise growth, very robust. LightGBM: leaf-wise growth + histogram binning → much faster on large data, risk of overfitting small data. CatBoost: best native categorical handling, ordered boosting reduces target leakage, good defaults.\n\n**Q94. 🟡 Random Forest vs XGBoost — which would you choose?**\nRF: fewer hyperparameters, robust, parallel, less overfitting — good quick baseline. XGBoost: usually higher accuracy on tabular data when tuned, handles imbalance (scale_pos_weight), but sensitive to hyperparameters.\n\n**Q95. 🔴 How is feature importance calculated in tree models? What are its pitfalls?**\nImpurity-based: total reduction in impurity from splits on that feature. Pitfalls: biased toward high-cardinality/continuous features, misleading with correlated features. Better: permutation importance or SHAP values.\n\n**Q96. 🟡 What is stacking?**\nTrain several diverse base models, then train a meta-model on their (out-of-fold) predictions to learn the best combination.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Unsupervised Learning\n\n**Q97. 🟢 Explain K-Means clustering.**\nInitialize k centroids, assign each point to the nearest centroid, update centroids to the mean of assigned points, repeat until assignments stop changing. Minimizes within-cluster sum of squares.\n\n**Q98. 🟡 How do you choose k in K-Means?**\nElbow method (plot inertia vs k, look for the elbow), silhouette score (maximize), gap statistic, and business interpretability (e.g., marketing can only handle 4 segments).\n\n**Q99. 🟡 Limitations of K-Means?**\nNeed to specify k, assumes spherical equal-size clusters, sensitive to initialization (use k-means++), outliers, and feature scale; only works with numeric data (use K-Modes/K-Prototypes for categorical).\n\n**Q100. 🟡 K-Means vs DBSCAN vs Hierarchical?**\nK-Means: fast, needs k, spherical clusters. DBSCAN: density-based, arbitrary shapes, detects noise/outliers, no k needed, struggles with varying density. Hierarchical: produces dendrogram, no k upfront, O(n²) memory — not for large data.\n\n**Q101. 🟡 What is PCA? How does it work?**\nDimensionality reduction that projects data onto orthogonal directions (principal components) capturing maximum variance. Steps: standardize, compute covariance matrix, eigen-decomposition, sort by eigenvalues, keep top k components.\n\n**Q102. 🟡 Why scale data before PCA?**\nPCA maximizes variance; features with larger scale (e.g., salary vs age) would dominate the components.\n\n**Q103. 🟡 How do you decide the number of principal components?**\nCumulative explained variance (e.g., keep 90–95%), scree plot elbow, or downstream model performance.\n\n**Q104. 🔴 PCA vs t-SNE vs LDA?**\nPCA: linear, unsupervised, preserves global variance, fast. t-SNE/UMAP: non-linear, preserves local neighborhoods, mainly for visualization. LDA: supervised, maximizes class separability.\n\n**Q105. 🟡 How would you evaluate a clustering model without labels?**\nSilhouette score, Davies-Bouldin index, Calinski-Harabasz, cluster stability, and most importantly business interpretability — profile clusters and check if they're actionable.\n\n**Q106. 🟡 How would you do anomaly detection?**\nStatistical (z-score, IQR), Isolation Forest (anomalies isolated in fewer splits), Local Outlier Factor, One-class SVM, autoencoders (high reconstruction error), or supervised if labels exist.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Evaluation Metrics\n\n**Q107. 🟢 What is a confusion matrix?**\nTable of TP, FP, FN, TN comparing predicted vs actual classes. Basis for precision, recall, accuracy, F1.\n\n**Q108. 🟢 Precision vs Recall — give examples where each matters more.**\nPrecision = TP/(TP+FP); matters when false positives are costly — spam filter (don't block important mail), recommending stocks. Recall = TP/(TP+FN); matters when false negatives are costly — cancer detection, fraud detection, airport security.\n\n**Q109. 🟡 Why is accuracy a bad metric for imbalanced data?**\nWith 99% negatives, predicting \"negative\" always gives 99% accuracy while catching zero positives. Use precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC.\n\n**Q110. 🟡 What is the F1-score? Why harmonic mean?**\nF1 = 2PR/(P+R). Harmonic mean punishes extreme values — if either precision or recall is low, F1 is low.\n\n**Q111. 🟡 Explain ROC curve and AUC.**\nROC plots TPR vs FPR at all thresholds. AUC = area under it = probability the model ranks a random positive higher than a random negative. 0.5 = random guessing, 1 = perfect. Threshold-independent.\n\n**Q112. 🔴 When would you prefer PR-AUC over ROC-AUC?**\nFor highly imbalanced data. ROC uses FPR = FP/(FP+TN), and with huge TN, FPR stays low even with many FPs → ROC looks optimistic. PR curve focuses on the positive class.\n\n**Q113. 🟡 How do you choose a classification threshold?**\nBased on business costs: assign costs to FP and FN, pick threshold that minimizes total cost or meets a constraint (e.g., recall ≥ 90%). Use PR curve, ROC (Youden's J), or capacity constraints (only 100 cases can be manually reviewed per day).\n\n**Q114. 🔴 What is model calibration?**\nWhether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies (of all cases predicted 0.7, ~70% should be positive). Check with reliability diagram / Brier score. Fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression. Important when probabilities drive decisions (expected revenue, risk pricing).\n\n**Q115. 🟡 What is log loss?**\n−[y log p + (1−y) log(1−p)] averaged. Heavily penalizes confident wrong predictions. Measures probability quality.\n\n**Q116. 🟡 Macro vs micro vs weighted averaging?**\nMacro: average metric per class equally (treats rare classes as important). Micro: aggregate TP/FP/FN globally (dominated by frequent classes). Weighted: per-class average weighted by support.\n\n**Q117. 🟡 What metrics would you use for a recommendation/ranking system?**\nOffline: Precision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, MRR, hit rate, coverage, diversity. Online: CTR, conversion, watch time, retention via A/B test.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering\n\n**Q118. 🟢 How do you handle missing data?**\nFirst understand why it's missing (MCAR/MAR/MNAR). Options: drop (if few rows/useless column), impute with mean/median/mode, KNN or iterative imputation, forward-fill for time series, add a missing indicator, or use models that handle NaN (XGBoost). Imputation statistics must be computed on training data only.\n\n**Q119. 🟡 What are MCAR, MAR and MNAR?**\nMCAR: missingness unrelated to any data (random sensor failure). MAR: related to other observed variables (older people skip an online question). MNAR: related to the missing value itself (high earners don't report income) — hardest to handle.\n\n**Q120. 🟡 One-hot encoding vs label encoding?**\nLabel encoding assigns integers — fine for ordinal data and tree models, but implies false order for nominal data in linear models. One-hot creates binary columns — for nominal data, but explodes dimensions with high cardinality.\n\n**Q121. 🔴 How do you handle high-cardinality categorical features (e.g., 10,000 zip codes)?**\nTarget/mean encoding (with K-fold and smoothing to avoid leakage), frequency encoding, grouping rare categories into \"Other\", hashing trick, embeddings (NN), or CatBoost's native handling. Also map to higher-level groups (zip → city/region).\n\n**Q122. 🟡 Standardization vs normalization? Which models need scaling?**\nStandardization: mean 0, SD 1 (robust-ish, for normally distributed features). Normalization: scales to [0,1] (sensitive to outliers). Need scaling: KNN, SVM, K-Means, PCA, linear/logistic with regularization, neural networks. Don't need: tree-based models.\n\n**Q123. 🟡 How do you handle imbalanced datasets?**\nProper metrics (PR-AUC, recall, F1), class weights, resampling (undersample majority, oversample minority, SMOTE — only on training data), threshold tuning, ensemble methods (balanced RF, EasyEnsemble), anomaly detection framing, collect more minority data.\n\n**Q124. 🔴 How does SMOTE work? What are its drawbacks?**\nFor each minority sample, pick one of its k nearest minority neighbors and create a synthetic sample on the line between them. Drawbacks: can create noisy samples in overlapping regions, doesn't work well with high dimensions or categorical data, must be applied after the train/test split (inside CV folds) to avoid leakage.\n\n**Q125. 🔴 What is data leakage? Give examples.**\nWhen training uses information unavailable at prediction time → unrealistically good validation results that fail in production. Examples: fitting scaler/imputer on full data before splitting; including a feature like \"account_closed_date\" when predicting churn; random split for time-series; same patient in train and test; target encoding without CV.\n\n**Q126. 🟡 What feature selection methods do you know?**\nFilter (correlation, chi-square, mutual information, variance threshold), wrapper (RFE, forward/backward selection), embedded (Lasso, tree importance), plus SHAP / permutation importance.\n\n**Q127. 🟡 Why is feature engineering important? Give examples.**\nGood features often matter more than model choice. Examples: from timestamp → hour, weekday, is_weekend; from transactions → RFM (recency, frequency, monetary); ratios (debt/income); aggregates (avg spend last 30 days); text length; distance from home location (fraud).\n\n**Q128. 🟡 How do you deal with skewed features?**\nLog, square root, Box-Cox, Yeo-Johnson transforms; binning; or use tree models that are insensitive to monotonic transforms.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Deep Learning\n\n**Q129. 🟢 What is a neural network? How does it learn?**\nLayers of neurons computing weighted sums plus non-linear activations. Learns by forward pass (prediction), computing loss, backpropagation (gradients via chain rule), and updating weights with gradient descent.\n\n**Q130. 🟡 Explain backpropagation.**\nAlgorithm to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight by applying the chain rule backward from output to input layer, reusing intermediate gradients for efficiency.\n\n**Q131. 🟡 Why do we need activation functions? Why is ReLU popular?**\nWithout non-linearity, a deep network is equivalent to a single linear layer. ReLU (max(0,x)) is cheap, doesn't saturate for positive inputs (reduces vanishing gradients), and gives sparse activations. Drawback: dying ReLU → Leaky ReLU.\n\n**Q132. 🟡 What is the vanishing gradient problem?**\nIn deep networks with sigmoid/tanh, gradients get multiplied by small numbers through many layers, becoming tiny → early layers don't learn. Solutions: ReLU, proper initialization (He/Xavier), batch normalization, residual connections, LSTM/GRU for sequences.\n\n**Q133. 🟡 What is dropout?**\nDuring training, randomly set a fraction of neurons to zero each step. Prevents co-adaptation, acts like training an ensemble of sub-networks → regularization. Disabled at inference (outputs scaled accordingly).\n\n**Q134. 🟡 What is batch normalization?**\nNormalizes layer inputs to zero mean, unit variance per mini-batch, then applies learnable scale & shift. Stabilizes and speeds up training, allows higher learning rates, slight regularization.\n\n**Q135. 🟡 SGD vs Adam?**\nSGD uses one global learning rate; with momentum it often generalizes well but needs tuning. Adam uses adaptive per-parameter learning rates with momentum (first and second moments) → converges faster, good default.\n\n**Q136. 🟡 What is a CNN? Why use it for images?**\nUses convolutional filters that slide across the image to detect local patterns, with parameter sharing and pooling. Far fewer parameters than fully connected networks, captures spatial hierarchy (edges→textures→objects), translation invariant.\n\n**Q137. 🟡 What is transfer learning?**\nReusing a model pretrained on a large dataset (ImageNet, BERT) for a new task — freeze early layers (general features), fine-tune later layers. Works well with little data and saves compute.\n\n**Q138. 🟡 RNN vs LSTM vs GRU?**\nRNN processes sequences with a hidden state but suffers vanishing gradients for long dependencies. LSTM adds a cell state and three gates (forget, input, output) to retain long-term information. GRU simplifies to two gates — faster, similar performance.\n\n**Q139. 🔴 Explain the Transformer and self-attention.**\nSelf-attention lets each token compute a weighted sum of all other tokens' values, with weights from the similarity of its query to their keys: softmax(QKᵀ/√d)V. Multi-head attention captures different relationships. Transformers add positional encodings, feed-forward layers, residual connections and layer norm. Advantages over RNN: parallel processing and better long-range dependencies.\n\n**Q140. 🟡 What is an epoch, batch, and iteration?**\nEpoch: one full pass through training data. Batch: subset of samples processed before one weight update. Iteration: one update step. Iterations per epoch = n / batch size.\n\n**Q141. 🟡 How would you deal with overfitting in a neural network?**\nMore data / augmentation, dropout, L2 weight decay, early stopping, smaller network, batch norm, transfer learning.\n\n**Q142. 🟡 What is an autoencoder?**\nNetwork trained to reconstruct its input through a narrow bottleneck: learns compressed representations. Uses: dimensionality reduction, denoising, anomaly detection (high reconstruction error).\n\n---\n\n## 12. NLP & GenAI\n\n**Q143. 🟢 Stemming vs lemmatization?**\nStemming chops word endings by rules (studies → studi), fast but crude. Lemmatization returns the dictionary base form using vocabulary/POS (studies → study, better → good), more accurate but slower.\n\n**Q144. 🟡 What is TF-IDF?**\nTerm Frequency × Inverse Document Frequency. Weights a word highly if it's frequent in a document but rare across the corpus, so common words like \"the\" get low weight.\n\n**Q145. 🟡 Bag-of-Words/TF-IDF vs Word Embeddings?**\nBoW/TF-IDF: sparse, high-dimensional, no semantic meaning or word order. Embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe): dense, low-dimensional, similar words have similar vectors. Contextual embeddings (BERT) give different vectors depending on context (\"bank\" of river vs money).\n\n**Q146. 🟡 How does Word2Vec work?**\nShallow neural network trained to predict context words from a word (Skip-gram) or a word from its context (CBOW). The learned hidden weights become word embeddings.\n\n**Q147. 🟡 BERT vs GPT?**\nBERT: encoder-only, bidirectional, trained with masked language modeling — best for understanding (classification, NER, QA). GPT: decoder-only, left-to-right autoregressive, trained on next-token prediction — best for generation.\n\n**Q148. 🟡 What is RAG?**\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation: split documents into chunks, embed them, store in a vector database; at query time, retrieve the most similar chunks and pass them to the LLM in the prompt. Grounds answers in up-to-date/private data, reduces hallucinations, no retraining needed.\n\n**Q149. 🟡 Fine-tuning vs RAG vs prompt engineering?**\nPrompt engineering: cheapest, no training, first thing to try. RAG: when the model needs external/changing knowledge. Fine-tuning: when you need to change behavior/style/format or domain-specific skills; LoRA makes it cheaper.\n\n**Q150. 🟡 What is hallucination in LLMs and how to reduce it?**\nModel generates plausible but false information. Reduce with RAG/grounding, lower temperature, asking it to cite sources, guardrails/verification steps, fine-tuning, and evaluation.\n\n**Q151. 🟡 What does temperature do in LLMs?**\nScales logits before softmax. Low temperature (→0) = deterministic, picks most likely tokens. High temperature = more random/creative outputs.\n\n**Q152. 🔴 How would you evaluate an LLM-based application?**\nTask-specific metrics (accuracy on labeled set, ROUGE/BLEU for summarization/translation), retrieval metrics for RAG (recall@k, context relevance), faithfulness/groundedness, human evaluation, LLM-as-a-judge with rubrics, latency and cost, and online metrics (user satisfaction, resolution rate).\n\n---\n\n## 13. Time Series\n\n**Q153. 🟢 What are the components of a time series?**\nTrend, seasonality, cyclic patterns, and random noise.\n\n**Q154. 🟡 What is stationarity and why does it matter?**\nStatistical properties (mean, variance, autocorrelation) constant over time. Models like ARIMA assume it. Test with ADF/KPSS; make stationary by differencing, log transform, or removing trend/seasonality.\n\n**Q155. 🟡 Explain ARIMA(p,d,q).**\nAR(p): regression on p lagged values. I(d): number of differences to make series stationary. MA(q): regression on q lagged forecast errors. Choose p from PACF, q from ACF. SARIMA adds seasonal terms.\n\n**Q156. 🟡 Why can't you use random K-fold cross-validation for time series?**\nIt leaks future information into training. Use time-based split / walk-forward (expanding or sliding window) validation.\n\n**Q157. 🟡 How would you use ML (e.g., XGBoost) for forecasting?**\nCreate lag features (sales t−1, t−7), rolling statistics (7-day mean), date features (day of week, month, holiday), external features (price, promotions); train with time-based validation; forecast recursively or directly per horizon.\n\n---\n\n## 14. SQL\n\n**Q158. 🟢 Difference between WHERE and HAVING?**\nWHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation (can use aggregates like COUNT).\n\n**Q159. 🟢 Types of joins?**\nINNER (matching rows), LEFT (all left + matches, NULL otherwise), RIGHT, FULL OUTER (all from both), CROSS (Cartesian product), SELF (table joined with itself).\n\n**Q160. 🟡 RANK vs DENSE_RANK vs ROW_NUMBER?**\nFor values 100,90,90,80: ROW_NUMBER → 1,2,3,4; RANK → 1,2,2,4; DENSE_RANK → 1,2,2,3.\n\n**Q161. 🟡 UNION vs UNION ALL?**\nUNION removes duplicates (slower), UNION ALL keeps all rows.\n\n**Q162. 🟡 Find the second highest salary.**\n```sql\nSELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees\nWHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);\n-- or\nSELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;\n```\n\n**Q163. 🟡 Top 3 highest-paid employees in each department.**\n```sql\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT name, dept, salary,\n         DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees)\nSELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rnk <= 3;\n```\n\n**Q164. 🟡 Find customers who never placed an order.**\n```sql\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.name\nFROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_id IS NULL;\n```\n\n**Q165. 🟡 Find duplicate emails.**\n```sql\nSELECT email, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM users GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n```\n\n**Q166. 🟡 Calculate running total of sales per user.**\n```sql\nSELECT user_id, order_date, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date) AS running_total\nFROM orders;\n```\n\n**Q167. 🟡 7-day rolling average of daily revenue.**\n```sql\nSELECT day, revenue,\n       AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS rolling_7d\nFROM daily_revenue;\n```\n\n**Q168. 🔴 Month-over-month revenue growth percentage.**\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders GROUP BY 1)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n             / LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month), 2) AS mom_growth_pct\nFROM monthly;\n```\n\n**Q169. 🔴 Users who logged in on 3 consecutive days.**\n```sql\nWITH d AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, login_date FROM logins),\ng AS (\n  SELECT user_id, login_date,\n         login_date - (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY login_date)) * INTERVAL '1 day' AS grp\n  FROM d)\nSELECT DISTINCT user_id FROM g GROUP BY user_id, grp HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3;\n```\n(Trick: date minus row number is constant for consecutive dates — \"gaps and islands\".)\n\n**Q170. 🔴 Calculate Day-7 retention by signup cohort.**\n```sql\nSELECT s.signup_date,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT s.user_id) AS signups,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) AS retained_d7,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT a.user_id) / COUNT(DISTINCT s.user_id), 2) AS d7_retention\nFROM signups s\nLEFT JOIN activity a\n  ON s.user_id = a.user_id AND a.activity_date = s.signup_date + INTERVAL '7 day'\nGROUP BY s.signup_date;\n```\n\n**Q171. 🟡 Click-through rate per ad using conditional aggregation.**\n```sql\nSELECT ad_id,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN action='click' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\n             / NULLIF(SUM(CASE WHEN action='view' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), 0), 2) AS ctr\nFROM ad_events GROUP BY ad_id;\n```\n\n**Q172. 🟡 COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) vs COUNT(DISTINCT col)?**\nCOUNT(*) counts all rows; COUNT(col) ignores NULLs; COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts unique non-null values.\n\n**Q173. 🟡 What is a CTE? Subquery vs CTE?**\nCommon Table Expression (WITH clause) — named temporary result set. More readable, reusable within the query, and supports recursion. Subqueries are inline; performance is usually similar.\n\n**Q174. 🟡 What is SQL query execution order?**\nFROM/JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → DISTINCT → ORDER BY → LIMIT.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Python & Pandas\n\n**Q175. 🟢 List vs tuple vs set vs dictionary?**\nList: ordered, mutable, allows duplicates. Tuple: ordered, immutable (can be dict key). Set: unordered, unique items, fast membership. Dict: key-value pairs, O(1) lookup.\n\n**Q176. 🟡 loc vs iloc?**\nloc: label-based (inclusive end). iloc: integer position-based (exclusive end).\n\n**Q177. 🟡 merge vs join vs concat in pandas?**\nmerge: SQL-style join on columns. join: joins on index. concat: stacks DataFrames along rows/columns.\n\n**Q178. 🟡 apply vs map vs vectorization?**\nmap: element-wise on Series. apply: function along rows/columns of DataFrame or Series. Vectorized NumPy/pandas operations are much faster than both — avoid loops/apply when possible.\n\n**Q179. 🟡 How do you handle a dataset too large to fit in memory?**\nRead in chunks (`chunksize`), select needed columns, downcast dtypes (float64→float32, category type), use Parquet, Dask/Polars/PySpark, or push computation to SQL/database.\n\n**Q180. 🟡 Shallow copy vs deep copy?**\nShallow copy creates a new outer object but references the same nested objects; deep copy recursively copies everything. In pandas, `df2 = df` is not a copy; use `df.copy()`.\n\n**Q181. 🟡 What is a generator? Why use it?**\nFunction using `yield` that produces items lazily one at a time — memory efficient for large data streams.\n\n**Q182. 🟡 Write code: find top 3 products by revenue per category in pandas.**\n```python\ndf['revenue'] = df['price'] * df['qty']\nagg = df.groupby(['category', 'product'], as_index=False)['revenue'].sum()\ntop3 = (agg.sort_values('revenue', ascending=False)\n           .groupby('category').head(3))\n```\n\n**Q183. 🟡 Write a function to compute precision and recall from scratch.**\n```python\ndef precision_recall(y_true, y_pred):\n    tp = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 1 and p == 1)\n    fp = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 0 and p == 1)\n    fn = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 1 and p == 0)\n    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp else 0\n    recall = tp / (tp + fn) if tp + fn else 0\n    return precision, recall\n```\n\n**Q184. 🔴 Implement K-Means from scratch (NumPy).**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\ndef kmeans(X, k, iters=100, seed=0):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    centroids = X[rng.choice(len(X), k, replace=False)]\n    for _ in range(iters):\n        dists = np.linalg.norm(X[:, None] - centroids[None], axis=2)\n        labels = dists.argmin(axis=1)\n        new = np.array([X[labels == j].mean(axis=0) if np.any(labels == j) else centroids[j]\n                        for j in range(k)])\n        if np.allclose(new, centroids):\n            break\n        centroids = new\n    return labels, centroids\n```\n\n**Q185. 🔴 Implement linear regression with gradient descent.**\n```python\ndef linear_regression_gd(X, y, lr=0.01, epochs=1000):\n    n, d = X.shape\n    w, b = np.zeros(d), 0.0\n    for _ in range(epochs):\n        y_hat = X @ w + b\n        err = y_hat - y\n        w -= lr * (2 / n) * (X.T @ err)\n        b -= lr * (2 / n) * err.sum()\n    return w, b\n```\n\n---\n\n## 16. MLOps / Deployment\n\n**Q186. 🟡 How would you deploy a machine learning model?**\nSerialize model (pickle/joblib/ONNX) → wrap in API (FastAPI/Flask) → containerize (Docker) → deploy on cloud/Kubernetes/serverless → add logging & monitoring → CI/CD for retraining. Or batch scoring via scheduled jobs (Airflow) if real-time isn't needed.\n\n**Q187. 🟡 What is data drift vs concept drift?**\nData drift: input feature distribution changes (new user demographics). Concept drift: relationship between features and target changes (fraudsters change tactics). Detect via PSI, KS tests, monitoring performance; fix by retraining.\n\n**Q188. 🟡 How do you monitor a model in production?**\nInput data quality & drift, prediction distribution, performance metrics when labels arrive, business KPIs, latency, errors, and alerting thresholds; scheduled or triggered retraining.\n\n**Q189. 🟡 What is training-serving skew?**\nFeatures computed differently in training vs production (different code/data), causing performance drop. Avoid with shared feature pipelines / feature store and logging production features.\n\n**Q190. 🟡 Batch vs real-time inference?**\nBatch: predictions precomputed periodically (daily churn scores) — cheaper, simpler. Real-time: on request with low latency (fraud check at payment) — needs fast features and model serving infra.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Project & Behavioral Technical Questions\n\n**Q191. Walk me through your best data science project end-to-end.**\nStructure: Problem & business impact → Data (source, size) → Cleaning/EDA findings → Feature engineering → Models tried (baseline first) → Evaluation metric and why → Results with numbers → Deployment/next steps → What you learned.\n\n**Q192. What was the biggest challenge in your project and how did you overcome it?**\nPick a real technical challenge (imbalanced data, leakage found, messy data, overfitting) and explain the diagnosis + solution + result.\n\n**Q193. Why did you choose that algorithm/metric?**\nJustify with data characteristics, business cost of errors, interpretability, and comparison with alternatives you tried.\n\n**Q194. How would you explain your model to a non-technical stakeholder?**\nAvoid jargon, focus on what decisions it supports, show top drivers (feature importance/SHAP) with intuitive examples, quantify business impact, and be clear about limitations.\n\n**Q195. Your model performs well offline but poorly in production. Why?**\nData leakage in training, training-serving skew, data/concept drift, different population (sampling bias), wrong validation split (random vs time), feedback loops, bugs in feature pipeline.\n\n**Q196. Your model has 99% accuracy. Should you be happy?**\nNot necessarily — check class imbalance (baseline accuracy), leakage, validation strategy, and business-relevant metrics (precision/recall).\n\n**Q197. How do you approach a new dataset?**\nUnderstand business context → check shape, types, missing values, duplicates → univariate distributions → bivariate relations with target → outliers → data quality issues → form hypotheses → baseline model.\n\n**Q198. How do you stay updated in data science?**\nMention specific blogs, papers, courses, Kaggle, newsletters, recent tools you tried (e.g., LLM/RAG projects).\n\n**Q199. Tell me about a time your analysis changed a decision.**\nUse STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result) with numbers.\n\n**Q200. What would you do in your first 90 days as a data scientist here?**\nLearn business & data, meet stakeholders, find quick wins, build trust with reliable analyses, then identify high-impact longer-term projects.\n\n---\n\n### Rapid-fire round (answer in one line)\n1. Can a model have high bias and high variance? → Yes (e.g., wrong model + small noisy data).\n2. Does feature scaling affect Random Forest? → No.\n3. Is logistic regression a linear classifier? → Yes, linear decision boundary.\n4. Default k-fold value? → 5 or 10.\n5. Does PCA require labels? → No.\n6. Is K-Means deterministic? → No, depends on initialization.\n7. What does a VIF of 1 mean? → No multicollinearity.\n8. What kind of loss for multi-class NN? → Categorical cross-entropy with softmax.\n9. Which is faster to train: RF or GBM? → RF (parallelizable).\n10. Can AUC be below 0.5? → Yes — model is worse than random (invert predictions).\n11. p-value = 0.03, α = 0.05? → Reject H₀.\n12. Is high recall always good? → No, may come with many false positives.\n13. What does `dropna()` do by default? → Drops rows with any NaN.\n14. What is an outlier-robust regression loss? → MAE / Huber.\n15. What's the output range of tanh? → (−1, 1).\n", "03_DS_Case_Study_Questions.md": "# Data Science Case Study Questions (Round 2)\n\n> **How to practice**: set a **25–30 min timer**, solve on paper *out loud* using the framework, then compare with the sample solution. The sample solutions are *one good answer*, not the only answer — interviewers value **structure, clarifying questions, and trade-offs** more than a \"correct\" answer.\n\n## Contents\n- [Part A — Frameworks (memorize these)](#part-a--frameworks)\n- [Part B — Product Metrics & Root-Cause Cases](#part-b--product-metrics--root-cause-analysis-cases)\n- [Part C — Machine Learning Modeling Cases](#part-c--machine-learning-modeling-cases)\n- [Part D — Experimentation / A/B Testing Cases](#part-d--experimentation--ab-testing-cases)\n- [Part E — Business Strategy & Guesstimate Cases](#part-e--business-strategy--guesstimate-cases)\n- [Part F — Extra Practice Cases (no solutions — try yourself)](#part-f--extra-practice-cases)\n- [Part G — Common Mistakes & Useful Phrases](#part-g--common-mistakes--useful-phrases)\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Frameworks\n\n### A1. Universal case-study framework (use for every case)\n```\n1. CLARIFY      → Restate problem. Ask: goal? business model? users? time frame? constraints? data available?\n2. STRUCTURE    → \"I'll approach this in N steps…\" (say it out loud)\n3. METRICS      → Define success metric precisely (numerator, denominator, time window) + guardrails\n4. HYPOTHESES / APPROACH → Break down (MECE), list hypotheses or modeling plan\n5. DATA         → What data/features do I need? Where does it come from? Quality issues?\n6. ANALYSIS / MODEL → Method, baseline, evaluation, validation\n7. RECOMMEND    → Decision + expected impact + risks + next steps / how to measure (A/B test)\n```\n\n### A2. ML System Design framework (for \"build a model to…\" cases)\n```\n1. Business objective    → What decision will the model drive? What is the value?\n2. ML problem framing    → Classification / regression / ranking / clustering? What is the label? Prediction horizon?\n3. Data                  → Sources, label definition, leakage risk, class imbalance, size\n4. Features              → User, item/product, behavior, time, context, aggregates\n5. Model                 → Baseline (rule / logistic) → advanced (XGBoost / NN). Why?\n6. Offline evaluation    → Metric tied to business cost, validation split (time-based!)\n7. Deployment            → Batch vs real-time, latency, threshold choice\n8. Online evaluation     → A/B test with business metric\n9. Monitoring & iteration→ Drift, retraining, feedback loops, fairness\n```\n\n### A3. Metric-drop / Root-cause framework (for \"metric X dropped 10%\")\n```\n1. Clarify the metric   → Definition? How much drop? Sudden or gradual? Since when? Compared to what (WoW, YoY)?\n2. Check data validity  → Tracking/logging bug? Pipeline delay? Definition change? Dashboard error?\n3. External factors     → Seasonality, holidays, competitor launch, news, regulation, outages, weather\n4. Internal factors     → Recent releases/features, marketing campaigns ended, pricing change, app update\n5. Segment the drop     → Platform (iOS/Android/web), app version, geography, new vs returning, device, channel, user cohort\n6. Decompose the metric → Metric = component × component (e.g., Revenue = Users × Conversion × AOV) — find which part dropped\n7. Conclude & act       → Root cause + fix + how to prevent / monitor\n```\n\n### A4. Metric selection framework\n- Use **AARRR funnel**: Acquisition → Activation → Retention → Referral → Revenue.\n- Or **HEART** (Google): Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task success.\n- Good metric = **measurable, sensitive, aligned with long-term value, hard to game**.\n- Always propose: **1 North Star metric + 2–3 supporting metrics + guardrail metrics**.\n\n### A5. Clarifying questions checklist\n- What is the business goal (growth, revenue, cost reduction, user satisfaction)?\n- Who are the users? What does the product do? How does the company make money?\n- What decision will be made based on this analysis?\n- What data do we have? How much history?\n- Any constraints (latency, interpretability, regulation, budget)?\n- What's the time frame?\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Product Metrics & Root-Cause Analysis Cases\n\n### Case B1. Daily Active Users (DAU) of our app dropped 15% yesterday. Investigate.\n\n**Clarify**\n- How is DAU defined (opened app vs performed action)? Drop vs yesterday, last week same day, or forecast?\n- Sudden (one day) or gradual? All markets or some?\n\n**Step 1 — Check data issues first**\n- Logging/tracking bug, new app release broke event firing, data pipeline delay (partial data), timezone change, definition change.\n\n**Step 2 — External factors**\n- Day of week / holiday / festival, major event (cricket final, elections), competitor launch, internet/cloud outage, app-store issue, news/PR crisis.\n\n**Step 3 — Internal factors**\n- New app version released? Server outage/latency increase? Login/OTP service failure? Push notifications stopped? Marketing campaign ended?\n\n**Step 4 — Segment**\n| Dimension | What a concentrated drop suggests |\n|---|---|\n| Platform (Android/iOS/web) | Platform-specific bug or release |\n| App version | Bug in new version |\n| Geography | Local outage, holiday, regulation |\n| New vs returning users | New ↓ → acquisition/marketing issue; returning ↓ → product/engagement issue |\n| Acquisition channel | Paid campaign stopped |\n| Device / OS version | Compatibility bug |\n\n**Step 5 — Decompose**: DAU = New users + Retained users + Resurrected users. Which component fell?\n\n**Conclusion example**: \"If the drop is only on Android version 5.2 released yesterday and concentrated on the login step, it's likely a login bug → roll back / hotfix, add monitoring alert on login success rate.\"\n\n---\n\n### Case B2. Revenue of an e-commerce company dropped 10% this month. Why?\n\n**Decompose revenue**:\n```\nRevenue = Visitors × Conversion rate × Average Order Value (AOV)\nAOV = Items per order × Avg price per item\n```\n- **Visitors ↓** → traffic source analysis (SEO, paid ads budget cut, app store ranking, seasonality).\n- **Conversion ↓** → funnel analysis: home → product page → add to cart → checkout → payment. Find the step with the biggest drop (e.g., payment gateway failures ↑).\n- **AOV ↓** → mix shift to cheaper categories, more discounts, fewer items per basket, removal of free-shipping threshold.\n\n**Also check**: returns/cancellations ↑, refunds, specific categories/regions, stock-outs of best-sellers, price changes vs competitors, last year's same month (seasonality, e.g., festive season last year).\n\n**Recommendation**: identify the component, quantify contribution of each factor (\"70% of drop due to conversion drop in payments step on mobile web\"), propose fix and monitoring.\n\n---\n\n### Case B3. How would you measure the success of a new feature — \"Stories\" on Instagram / \"Reels\" on a social app?\n\n**Clarify goal**: increase engagement & time spent, content creation, retention, compete with competitor.\n\n**Metrics**\n- **Adoption**: % of DAU who view a story; % who post a story.\n- **Engagement**: stories viewed per user per day, time spent on stories, completion rate, replies/reactions.\n- **Creation**: stories posted per creator, % of users who are creators.\n- **Retention**: D7/D30 retention of users who adopt vs not (careful: selection bias → use A/B test).\n- **North star**: total time spent / meaningful interactions per DAU.\n- **Guardrails / cannibalization**: feed posts, feed time, ad revenue (ads in feed), app performance (load time), unfollows/reports.\n\n**Measurement**: A/B test with user-level randomization; watch for network effects (stories are seen by friends) → cluster randomization; novelty effect → run 3–4 weeks.\n\n---\n\n### Case B4. Uber/Ola — rider wait times increased in one city. Investigate.\n\n- Clarify: average or P90 wait time? Since when? All areas or specific zones/times?\n- **Supply side**: fewer active drivers (driver incentive change, fuel price hike, competitor offering better pay, festival), drivers concentrated elsewhere.\n- **Demand side**: demand surge (event, rain, office reopening, metro strike).\n- **Matching/tech**: dispatch algorithm change, map/GPS issues, ETA calculation bug.\n- Segment: by zone, hour, car type, new vs experienced drivers.\n- Metrics to look at: supply hours, requests, acceptance rate, cancellation rate, surge multiplier, driver utilization.\n- Actions: targeted driver incentives in high-demand zones/times, surge pricing tuning, repositioning suggestions to drivers.\n\n---\n\n### Case B5. What metrics would you track for a food delivery app (Swiggy/Zomato)? Define a North Star.\n\n- **North Star**: weekly orders delivered successfully (or GMV), balanced with customer retention.\n- **Customer**: orders per user, repeat rate, D30 retention, conversion (app open → order), AOV, NPS/ratings, cancellation rate.\n- **Delivery**: average delivery time, % on-time, delivery partner utilization, cost per delivery.\n- **Restaurant**: # active restaurants, acceptance rate, prep time, restaurant churn.\n- **Financial**: take rate, contribution margin per order, CAC, LTV/CAC.\n- **Guardrails**: complaint rate, refund rate, food quality ratings.\n- Mention trade-offs: faster delivery ↔ cost; discounts ↑ orders but ↓ margin.\n\n---\n\n### Case B6. Engagement on LinkedIn/our platform is increasing, but the number of new posts is decreasing. What's happening?\n\n- Clarify definitions: engagement = likes/comments/time? posts = original posts only?\n- Hypotheses:\n  - Fewer creators but popular ones getting more engagement (concentration) → check posts per creator, creator count.\n  - Feed algorithm change promoting viral/older content or videos.\n  - Shift from posting to commenting/resharing (new feature).\n  - Spam filter change removing posts.\n  - Seasonality (holiday → people consume more, create less).\n- Segment by creator type, region, content type.\n- Risk: long-term health — consumption depends on creation; declining creators hurts future engagement. Recommend: creator incentives, notifications on post performance, track creator retention.\n\n---\n\n### Case B7. Funnel analysis — a fintech app's loan application completion rate dropped. How do you analyze?\n\n- Map the funnel: app open → loan page → eligibility check → KYC upload → bank details → submit → approval → disbursal.\n- Compute conversion at each step; compare with previous period; find the step with the largest drop.\n- Segment by device, app version, region, user credit profile, channel.\n- Check: KYC verification provider downtime, new mandatory field added, OTP failures, regulation change, interest rate change.\n- Qualitative: session recordings, customer support tickets, user surveys.\n- Fix and validate with A/B test (e.g., simplified KYC flow).\n\n---\n\n## Part C — Machine Learning Modeling Cases\n\n### Case C1. Build a customer churn prediction model for a telecom / subscription company.\n\n**1. Business objective**\nIdentify customers likely to churn in the next 30 days so the retention team can offer incentives. Goal: reduce churn rate while controlling incentive cost.\n\n**2. Problem framing**\n- Binary classification. **Label**: churned = didn't renew / cancelled within next 30 days after the snapshot date.\n- Define churn clearly (contractual: cancellation; non-contractual like e-commerce: no purchase in 90 days).\n- Prediction made monthly for all active customers (batch).\n\n**3. Data & features**\n| Category | Features |\n|---|---|\n| Demographics | Age, location, tenure |\n| Account | Plan type, contract length, payment method, price, discounts |\n| Usage | Calls/data usage trends (last 7/30/90 days), % change in usage, last active date |\n| Engagement | Logins, app usage, feature usage |\n| Support | # complaints, tickets, resolution time, network issues |\n| Billing | Late payments, bill shock, price increases |\n| Competitive | Competitor offers in region |\n- Strong signals: **usage declining trend**, complaints, contract ending soon.\n- **Avoid leakage**: only use data available before the snapshot date (e.g., not \"cancellation request call\").\n\n**4. Model**\n- Baseline: logistic regression (interpretable).\n- Main: XGBoost/LightGBM; handle imbalance (churn ~5–20%) with class weights.\n- Time-based train/validation split (train on Jan–Jun snapshots, validate on Jul).\n\n**5. Evaluation**\n- PR-AUC, recall at top-K% (retention team can call only top 10%), lift chart (top decile lift).\n- Business: choose threshold where expected profit is max:\n  `Profit = Σ [P(churn) × P(saved | offer) × CLV − cost of offer]`\n\n**6. Beyond prediction — uplift modeling**\n- Predicting churn ≠ knowing who will respond to an offer. Segment users into: persuadables, sure things, lost causes, sleeping dogs. **Uplift models** target persuadables.\n\n**7. Deployment & measurement**\n- Monthly batch scores to CRM. Explain reasons using SHAP (\"usage dropped 40%, 3 complaints\").\n- A/B test: treatment group gets retention offers based on model, control gets business-as-usual → measure churn rate difference and ROI.\n- Monitor drift, retrain monthly/quarterly.\n\n---\n\n### Case C2. Design a credit card fraud detection system.\n\n**Clarify**: real-time at transaction time? Cost of FN (fraud loss) vs FP (blocked genuine customer → bad experience)? Latency limit (~100ms)?\n\n**Framing**: binary classification, extremely imbalanced (~0.1% fraud). Labels from chargebacks (delayed by weeks → label delay!).\n\n**Features**\n- Transaction: amount, merchant category, online/offline, time of day, country.\n- **Behavioral/velocity**: # transactions in last 1 hr/24 hr, amount vs user's average, new merchant, new device, distance from last transaction (impossible travel), time since last txn.\n- Device/IP: new device, IP risk score, VPN.\n- Merchant risk: historical fraud rate of merchant.\n- Graph features: shared devices/cards across accounts.\n\n**Model**\n- Rules engine (fast, interpretable) + ML model (XGBoost) + anomaly detection (Isolation Forest/autoencoder) for new fraud patterns.\n- Imbalance: class weights, undersampling non-fraud, focus on PR-AUC.\n\n**Decisioning (3 tiers)**\n- Score < low threshold → approve.\n- Middle → step-up authentication (OTP) — reduces friction cost of FP.\n- High → block + manual review.\n- Thresholds chosen by cost: minimize `FN × avg fraud amount + FP × customer friction cost` subject to review team capacity.\n\n**Evaluation**: precision, recall, PR-AUC, $ fraud caught, false decline rate, latency.\n\n**Challenges**: concept drift (fraudsters adapt) → frequent retraining, monitoring; adversarial behavior; label delay; feedback loop (blocked transactions have no label → use small random exploration/holdout).\n\n---\n\n### Case C3. Design a recommendation system for an e-commerce site / Netflix.\n\n**Clarify**: Where are recommendations shown (home page, \"similar items\", email)? Goal: CTR, purchases, watch time, diversity? Scale (millions of users & items)?\n\n**Data**: implicit feedback (views, clicks, add-to-cart, purchases, watch %), explicit (ratings), item metadata (category, price, brand, text, images), user profile, context (time, device).\n\n**Architecture (2-stage)**\n1. **Candidate generation** (millions → ~500): collaborative filtering (matrix factorization / ALS), item-to-item similarity (\"bought together\"), content-based embeddings, popular/trending, two-tower neural model + approximate nearest neighbor search.\n2. **Ranking** (500 → top 20): gradient boosted trees or deep model predicting P(click/purchase) with user, item, user-item interaction & context features.\n3. **Re-ranking**: diversity, freshness, business rules (in stock, remove purchased items), fairness.\n\n**Cold start**\n- New user: popular/trending items, onboarding preferences, location/demographics, session-based recs.\n- New item: content features (text/image embeddings), exploration boost.\n\n**Evaluation**\n- Offline: Precision@K, Recall@K, NDCG, MAP, coverage, diversity (time-based split).\n- Online A/B test: CTR, conversion, revenue per user, watch time, long-term retention; guardrails: latency, diversity.\n\n**Pitfalls**: popularity bias, filter bubbles, feedback loops (recommendations influence future training data) → exploration (bandits).\n\n---\n\n### Case C4. Predict house prices / car prices for a listing platform.\n\n- Framing: regression. Target: final sale price (log-transform because skewed).\n- Features: area, bedrooms, bathrooms, age, floor, location (lat/long, locality, distance to metro/schools/offices), amenities, furnishing, neighborhood average price (**careful with leakage — compute from past data only**), listing text (NLP), images (CNN embeddings), market trends (time).\n- Data issues: outliers (typos: 1,000,000 sq ft), missing values, duplicate listings.\n- Models: linear regression baseline → Random Forest / XGBoost / LightGBM.\n- Metrics: MAE (interpretable in ₹), MAPE (relative), RMSE; evaluate per segment (luxury vs budget).\n- Validation: time-based split (prices change with time); spatial leakage — same building in train & test.\n- Product: show price range (prediction intervals via quantile regression), explain drivers to users.\n\n---\n\n### Case C5. A bank wants to predict loan default (credit risk scoring).\n\n- Framing: binary classification — default = 90+ days past due within 12 months.\n- Features: credit bureau score, income, debt-to-income ratio, employment type/tenure, existing loans, repayment history, credit utilization, loan amount/tenure, banking transactions (salary credits, bounced cheques).\n- Constraints: **regulation & explainability** → logistic regression scorecard (WOE binning) or XGBoost with SHAP explanations; **fairness** — no protected attributes (gender, religion), check proxy bias.\n- **Reject inference**: we only have outcomes for approved applicants → biased training data.\n- Metrics: AUC, Gini (2·AUC − 1), KS statistic, calibration (probability used for pricing), expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD.\n- Decision: approval threshold chosen to balance approval rate vs default rate; risk-based pricing.\n- Monitoring: PSI for drift, vintage analysis of defaults.\n\n---\n\n### Case C6. Forecast demand for products in a retail / quick-commerce (Blinkit/Zepto) warehouse.\n\n- Clarify: granularity (SKU × store × day/hour), horizon (next 7 days), purpose (inventory replenishment), cost of stock-out vs overstock (perishables!).\n- Features: historical sales lags (t−1, t−7, t−14), rolling means, day of week, holidays/festivals, weather, promotions/discounts, price, events, stock-out flags (sales = 0 because out of stock, not no demand → **censored demand**).\n- Models: seasonal naive baseline → Prophet / SARIMA for top SKUs → global LightGBM model across SKUs → DL (TFT/DeepAR) at scale.\n- Metrics: WAPE / MAPE, bias (over vs under forecasting), service level; asymmetric loss (quantile loss — forecast P80 for perishables trade-off).\n- Validation: walk-forward backtesting.\n- Business output: reorder quantities = forecast + safety stock.\n\n---\n\n### Case C7. Build a model to detect fake reviews / spam on a platform.\n\n- Labels: manually labeled reviews, user reports, known spammer accounts; possibly weak labels.\n- Features: text (TF-IDF, BERT embeddings, sentiment extremity, duplicate/near-duplicate text), reviewer behavior (account age, # reviews/day, all 5-star, reviews only one seller), product-level (sudden burst of reviews), network (groups of accounts reviewing same products, shared IP/device).\n- Models: text classifier + behavioral GBM + graph-based detection (collusion rings).\n- Metrics: precision (falsely removing genuine reviews hurts trust) + recall; human review queue for borderline cases.\n- Adversarial → continual retraining, monitoring.\n\n---\n\n### Case C8. Customer segmentation for a marketing team.\n\n- Goal: personalized campaigns.\n- Data: RFM (Recency, Frequency, Monetary), product categories bought, channel preference, demographics, engagement with emails, discount sensitivity.\n- Approach: feature scaling → K-Means / GMM (or RFM rule-based segments as baseline), choose k using silhouette + business interpretability (4–6 segments).\n- Profile segments: \"High-value loyalists\", \"Discount seekers\", \"At-risk churners\", \"New users\", \"Dormant\".\n- Action per segment + measure via A/B test of targeted vs generic campaign.\n- Re-cluster periodically; track segment migration.\n\n---\n\n### Case C9. Design a system to auto-classify customer support tickets (NLP / LLM).\n\n- Goal: route tickets to right team, reduce resolution time.\n- Data: historical tickets with assigned category; class imbalance; messy multilingual text.\n- Approach: baseline TF-IDF + logistic regression → fine-tuned BERT/DistilBERT → or LLM zero/few-shot classification with RAG over help docs to also draft replies.\n- Metrics: macro-F1, accuracy per category, % auto-routed correctly; business: first-response time, resolution time, CSAT.\n- Deployment: confidence threshold — low confidence goes to human; human corrections feed retraining.\n- LLM-specific: hallucination guardrails, PII masking, cost/latency per ticket.\n\n---\n\n### Case C10. Predict the ETA for a food delivery order.\n\n- Components: restaurant prep time + driver assignment time + pickup travel time + delivery travel time.\n- Features: restaurant historical prep time by hour, order size, current restaurant load, distance, traffic, weather, driver location/availability, time of day, day of week.\n- Model: regression (LightGBM) per component or end-to-end; quantile regression for P90 (promise times).\n- Metric: MAE, % orders within ±5 min, **asymmetric cost** — late is worse than early (customer dissatisfaction) → tune bias.\n- Online: A/B test impact on conversion (shown ETA affects ordering!), cancellation, ratings.\n\n---\n\n## Part D — Experimentation / A/B Testing Cases\n\n### Case D1. We changed the \"Buy Now\" button color. Conversion went from 10% to 10.5%. Should we launch?\n\n**Clarify**: Sample size per group? Test duration? Was it pre-registered?\n\n**Analysis**\n- Two-proportion z-test. Suppose n = 20,000 per group:\n  - p_pool = 0.1025, SE = √(0.1025×0.8975×(2/20000)) ≈ 0.00303\n  - z = 0.005/0.00303 ≈ 1.65 → p ≈ 0.10 (two-tailed) → **not significant** at α=0.05.\n- Check power: to detect 0.5% absolute lift at 10% baseline need n ≈ 16×0.09/0.000025 ≈ **57,600 per group**. Test is underpowered.\n- Check SRM, duration ≥ 1 week, novelty effect, segments, guardrails (revenue, AOV).\n\n**Recommendation**: continue running until planned sample size is reached; don't launch yet based on this data. Also: practical significance — 0.5% lift on large traffic could be valuable; low cost change.\n\n---\n\n### Case D2. Design an A/B test for a new recommendation algorithm on an e-commerce homepage.\n\n1. **Hypothesis**: new algorithm increases purchases per user.\n2. **Metrics**: primary — revenue per user or conversion rate; secondary — CTR on recs, items per order; guardrails — page latency, return rate, diversity, bounce rate.\n3. **Unit**: user (logged-in) → consistent experience.\n4. **Sample size**: from baseline conversion & variance, MDE (e.g., 1% relative), α=0.05, power 0.8; revenue metric is high-variance → use CUPED or trim outliers.\n5. **Duration**: ≥ 2 weeks (weekly cycles), avoid sale events.\n6. **Ramp-up**: 1% → 5% → 50% to catch bugs.\n7. **Analysis**: t-test on revenue per user, CI, segment by new/returning users, device.\n8. **Pitfalls**: novelty effect, interference minimal here, multiple metrics → correct.\n9. **Decision**: launch if primary improves significantly with no guardrail violations and cost is justified.\n\n---\n\n### Case D3. A/B test shows the treatment increased clicks but decreased revenue. What do you do?\n\n- Verify data (SRM, bugs, outliers).\n- Understand mechanism: clickbait-y recommendations? Users clicking more but finding less relevant items? Cheaper items promoted?\n- Look at full funnel: click → add to cart → purchase; AOV; segments.\n- Decide based on company's **primary goal (OEC)** — usually revenue/long-term value beats clicks. Clicks are a proxy; when proxy and true goal disagree, trust the true goal.\n- Recommend: don't launch; iterate on the algorithm (optimize for purchase probability, not CTR).\n\n---\n\n### Case D4. How would you test a new pricing strategy for a ride-sharing app?\n\n- Problem: user-level randomization in marketplaces causes **interference** — treatment pricing changes driver supply available to control users; also fairness concerns (two users seeing different prices at same location — PR risk).\n- Options:\n  - **Geo-experiment**: randomize cities/regions (fewer units → lower power; use matched markets, synthetic control).\n  - **Switchback test**: alternate pricing for the whole city by time windows (e.g., every 30 min, randomized).\n- Metrics: rides completed, revenue, driver earnings, rider conversion, wait time, cancellation, long-term rider retention.\n- Analysis: account for autocorrelation in time windows; run several weeks.\n\n---\n\n### Case D5. You can't run an A/B test (e.g., a TV ad campaign launched nationally). How do you measure its impact?\n\n- **Pre-post with control**: Difference-in-Differences if campaign ran in some regions only.\n- **Synthetic control**: build a weighted combination of non-exposed regions to mimic the treated one.\n- **Interrupted time series / causal impact (Bayesian structural time series)**: forecast what would have happened without the campaign, compare to actual.\n- **Marketing Mix Modeling (MMM)**: regression of sales on spend across channels with adstock & saturation effects.\n- Check confounders: seasonality, competitor activity, price changes.\n\n---\n\n### Case D6. The A/B test result is significant overall, but negative for mobile users and positive for desktop. What do you do?\n\n- Check if segment sizes are adequate (small segments → noisy; multiple comparisons).\n- Check for Simpson's paradox and whether randomization was balanced across devices.\n- Investigate a mobile-specific bug/UX issue (layout, load time).\n- Options: launch on desktop only, fix mobile and re-test, or launch overall if mobile negative effect is small and not significant after correction.\n\n---\n\n## Part E — Business Strategy & Guesstimate Cases\n\n### Case E1. Estimate the number of cups of tea/coffee sold daily in Bangalore.\n```\nPopulation ≈ 13 million\nAdults (15+) ≈ 75% → ~10M\nTea/coffee drinkers ≈ 80% → 8M\nAvg cups per day per drinker ≈ 2 → 16M cups\nBought outside (vs home) ≈ 30% → ~5M cups/day sold\n```\nState assumptions clearly, sanity check, and mention how you'd refine (survey, segment by office-goers/students).\n\n### Case E2. Estimate the market size for an online grocery delivery app in a Tier-1 Indian city.\n- Households → % with smartphones & internet → % who shop groceries online → orders/month → AOV → monthly GMV.\n- Example: 4M households × 60% smartphone/online-capable × 25% adoption × 4 orders/month × ₹600 ≈ **₹144 crore/month**.\n\n### Case E3. Swiggy wants to launch a subscription program (like Swiggy One). How would you evaluate it?\n- Goals: increase order frequency & retention, lock-in vs competitors.\n- Economics: subscription fee revenue + incremental orders × margin − cost of free deliveries/discounts − cannibalization (users who'd order anyway).\n- Analysis: identify target users (frequent orderers vs medium — medium users have most uplift potential), price sensitivity (survey/price testing), break-even order frequency.\n- Experiment: offer to random subset → measure incremental orders per user, retention, net margin over 2–3 months.\n- Metrics: subscription uptake, renewal rate, orders/subscriber vs control, contribution margin, LTV.\n\n### Case E4. Should an e-commerce company offer free shipping above ₹499 instead of ₹999?\n- Trade-off: ↑ conversion & orders vs ↑ shipping cost and possibly ↓ AOV (users stop adding items to reach ₹999).\n- Data: distribution of cart values (how many orders between ₹499–999), shipping cost per order, conversion lift estimates, cart abandonment due to shipping fees.\n- Run A/B test (user-level) → primary metric: **net profit per user**; secondary: conversion, AOV, orders/user.\n- Recommend based on profit, not just orders.\n\n### Case E5. A company's marketing team wants to know which channel gives the best ROI.\n- Define ROI = (incremental revenue − cost)/cost.\n- Attribution: last-click (biased), first-click, linear, time-decay, data-driven (Shapley/Markov) attribution.\n- Better: incrementality via holdout experiments (geo lift tests, ghost ads) + Marketing Mix Modeling.\n- Metrics: CAC per channel, LTV of acquired users (quality matters, not just volume), payback period.\n\n### Case E6. A bank sees rising credit card defaults. What would you analyze and recommend?\n- Clarify time period, product, portfolio segment.\n- Vintage analysis: are recent cohorts (newly approved) defaulting more? → approval policy/model drift.\n- Segment: income band, region, channel (online vs branch), employment type, credit limit.\n- External: macroeconomy (layoffs, inflation, interest rate hikes).\n- Check model: PSI drift in applicant population, calibration of risk model.\n- Actions: tighten approval cut-off for risky segments, lower limits, early warning model for collections, retrain risk model.\n\n---\n\n## Part F — Extra Practice Cases\n\n> Solve these yourself using the frameworks. Time each one (25 min).\n\n**Product / Metrics**\n1. YouTube watch time dropped 5% week-over-week. Investigate.\n2. How would you measure the success of WhatsApp's \"Communities\" feature?\n3. Define metrics for Google Maps' \"Live traffic\" feature.\n4. Number of Uber drivers increased, but completed rides stayed flat. Why?\n5. Spotify: how would you decide whether to show a \"Daily Mix\" on the home screen?\n6. App-store rating of our app dropped from 4.5 to 3.9 in a month. What's happening?\n7. Average session time increased but retention decreased. Good or bad?\n8. Amazon Prime sign-ups dropped after a price increase. How would you analyze the impact?\n\n**Machine Learning**\n9. Build a model to predict which customers will respond to a marketing email.\n10. Predict hospital patient readmission within 30 days.\n11. Design a search ranking system for an e-commerce site.\n12. Detect fake accounts on a social media platform.\n13. Predict the optimal price for a hotel room (dynamic pricing).\n14. Build a model to predict employee attrition for HR.\n15. Detect defective products from images on a manufacturing line.\n16. Predict insurance claim fraud.\n17. Build a lead-scoring model for a B2B sales team.\n18. Build an LLM-powered chatbot that answers questions from company policy documents — how would you design and evaluate it?\n19. Predict which users will convert from free trial to paid.\n20. Predict the click-through rate of ads (CTR prediction).\n\n**Experimentation**\n21. Design an experiment to test whether adding video reviews increases sales.\n22. You ran 10 variants of a landing page; one is significant at p=0.04. Launch it?\n23. How would you A/B test a new feature in a social network where users interact?\n24. An experiment's results are very different in week 1 and week 3. Why?\n\n**Business / Guesstimates**\n25. Estimate the number of ATMs in India.\n26. Estimate monthly revenue of a popular café near your college.\n27. Should Zomato enter the grocery delivery business?\n28. How would you decide where to open the next store for a retail chain?\n29. Estimate how many Ola/Uber rides happen in Mumbai per day.\n30. Netflix is considering a cheaper ad-supported plan. How would you evaluate it?\n\n---\n\n## Part G — Common Mistakes & Useful Phrases\n\n### ❌ Mistakes to avoid\n- Jumping into a solution without clarifying questions.\n- Choosing accuracy for imbalanced problems.\n- Ignoring data leakage and time-based validation.\n- Proposing deep learning first without a baseline.\n- Forgetting the **business decision** and **how success is measured** (A/B test).\n- Going silent — think aloud.\n- Listing 20 hypotheses without prioritizing; prioritize by likelihood × impact × ease of checking.\n- Ignoring trade-offs (cost of FP vs FN, latency, interpretability, fairness).\n\n### ✅ Useful phrases\n- \"Before I dive in, let me ask a few clarifying questions…\"\n- \"Let me structure my approach into four parts…\"\n- \"I'd first rule out data/instrumentation issues, since that's the most common cause of sudden drops.\"\n- \"Let me define the metric precisely: numerator is…, denominator is…, over a 7-day window.\"\n- \"I'd start with a simple, interpretable baseline so we have a benchmark.\"\n- \"The key trade-off here is between precision and recall, because a false negative costs us X while a false positive costs Y.\"\n- \"To validate this in production, I'd run an A/B test with … as the primary metric and … as guardrails.\"\n- \"If I had more time, I'd also look at…\"\n- \"To summarize: the recommendation is…, expected impact…, risks…, and next steps…\"\n\n### Final 60-second summary template (end every case with this)\n> \"To summarize, the problem was **[X]**. I'd measure success using **[metric]**. My approach is **[analysis/model]**, using **[key data/features]**. The main risks are **[leakage/bias/cost trade-off]**, which I'd handle by **[mitigation]**. I'd validate impact with **[A/B test / backtest]** and monitor **[drift/guardrails]** after launch.\"\n"};
